insert into public.chunks (id, episode_id, cms_item_id, chunk_index, content, token_estimate, speakers, start_timestamp, end_timestamp, podcast_index, episode_name, slug, web_url, guest_name, guest_title, categories, short_description, podcast_length)
select id, episode_id, cms_item_id, chunk_index, content, token_estimate, speakers, start_timestamp, end_timestamp, podcast_index, episode_name, slug, web_url, guest_name, guest_title, categories, short_description, podcast_length
from jsonb_to_recordset($jsonpayload$
[{"id": "0d47315a-41ad-4ede-8fde-49ed607939d8", "episode_id": "58fd0630-891d-4c4d-a89d-96aad5a6ae04", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4856", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Cole Heilborn (26:51): Wow. Okay. So you just brought up something that I feel like we could talk a lot more about. I'm curious to dive into this. I one, how do, you said you use this algorithm for all of your clients, for all of the different mediums out there. One, how do you compare print versus social versus digital, and how do you, I guess it sounds like you're ranking them somehow. And what's the goal with the algorithm? Is it to show the quality of the PR hit or I guess what's its purpose?", "token_estimate": 126, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "26:51", "end_timestamp": "26:51", "podcast_index": 51, "episode_name": "Ep 51: Cory Van Auken | Digital Marketing and PR | Darby Communications", "slug": "ep-51", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-51", "guest_name": "Cory Van Auken", "guest_title": "Darby Communications", "categories": [], "short_description": "We go deep inside Darby's approach to analyze how they integrate digital and PR", "podcast_length": "44:73"}, {"id": "c976b0ea-f3c0-4793-a9a9-d3892d2ed902", "episode_id": "58fd0630-891d-4c4d-a89d-96aad5a6ae04", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4856", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Cory Van Auken (27:25): Yeah, so the purpose is a visual representation of the value. And so each piece of coverage, it's not compared directly to each other. So we're not comparing which is better, a direct hit digital piece of coverage or a print piece of coverage. They do different things. So what we're trying to show is what the purpose of each piece of coverage is, and they're different according to the medium that we're working with and the size of the audience. So like I said, we collect all sorts of metrics. A lot of this is proprietary, but I can explain some of the metrics would be coverage type or category. So is it a print, is it digital? Is it social coverage type, is it a review or is it a brand story? So a review of a product or a review of a brand story. Those do different things for people. (28:19): Is it a roundup as it just to mention, then we can look at it's named differently depending on what the medium is, but the audience size for lack of a better term, so the circulation number, the reach number, unique visitors per month, we look at those, the type of link, is it a direct link? Is it affiliate link? Is there no link? How long the feature is, the domain authority from the site that you're getting referrals from, whether or not the brand had their name and the title of the story that has an increased impact. (29:00): All of these things we look at. And the purpose of this then is each piece of coverage spits out two numbers and they're coordinates. So they spit out an X number and a Y number, and those coordinates are used to graph onto the communicators funnel, which is very similar to a sales funnel, but the levels are slightly different. So if you can imagine a funnel, the top piece of the funnel, excuse me, in the communicators funnel would be a brand impact. And then if you move down into the funnel, you have a digital impact, which I know Chris spoke a little bit about in terms of the SEO impact. So we'll look at that also backlink impact and referral traffic impact. And then the final piece of that funnel, the lowest level would be bottom line impact. Did this lead to sales, direct sales or assisted conversions or stuff like that. So that is the y value of each piece of coverage. Where does it fall in the funnel, (29:57): Top, middle, bottom. And then the X value is for lack of a better term, an audience score. And that shows what type of audience is this. Is this a broader audience like a men's journal, which I don't know what their circulation numbers, but I think it's in the millions, like 3 million for example. Is this a really broad audience that is great for brand awareness and getting tons of eyeballs on your product or on your brand, but we'll take some time to usher those people through their journey. So they're in the awareness stage, you need to get them into the consideration phase next and then the acquisition phase and all of that, or is this audience super niche and there's a lot of value, and I think people sometimes mistakenly think that the smaller the publication is, the less value. Sometimes there is because you're talking to less people. That's not necessarily the case, especially as you said, if there's some publications that have a really niche audience, (31:07): And let's say, this is a crazy example, but I'm just going to go with it. There is a publication that only writes about red water bottles. If you're a brand that makes a red water bottle, well that's really valuable to get coverage in there. And then you compare that to coverage in men's journal and those, you can't really compare them directly, but that red water bottle hit can be really important. So that's where the audience score comes in. It's the lateral left or right, so it's graphed onto this funnel. And that was a pain to get it to be able to graph into a funnel shape, it was hard to do. And then in our reports, we transpose that into our reports. That's only part of it. And to really show the value of each piece of coverage. Now, this went through many iterations and before we started to think about it in the term of just a singular score. However, when you do, and it was a scale of one to a hundred, but when you do that, you automatically think the higher the score, the better the piece of coverage. (32:09): And again, I think we think that's a flawed way of thinking about it because pieces of coverage do different things. And so we went away from the spectrum and instead we went into the funnel shape and we found that our clients are, use it in a way that they're able to quickly and easily decipher, okay, this piece of coverage is in this category. It's a niche brand awareness piece. And then they can determine within their marketing teams, well, how can we use, and we help to do this as well, how can we use this piece of coverage to amplify it even more? Does that make some sense?", "token_estimate": 1224, "speakers": ["Cory Van Auken"], "start_timestamp": "27:25", "end_timestamp": "27:25", "podcast_index": 51, "episode_name": "Ep 51: Cory Van Auken | Digital Marketing and PR | Darby Communications", "slug": "ep-51", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-51", "guest_name": "Cory Van Auken", "guest_title": "Darby Communications", "categories": [], "short_description": "We go deep inside Darby's approach to analyze how they integrate digital and PR", "podcast_length": "44:73"}, {"id": "f95158d8-1d3d-4e12-98d1-0b12cf3abdf7", "episode_id": "58fd0630-891d-4c4d-a89d-96aad5a6ae04", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4856", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Cole Heilborn (32:53): Yeah. So I guess a clarifying question, what is the goal then of being able to plant these plots on this visual that you have? What are you able to do with that? Because you can see where the audience is in terms of their focus and where they are in terms of their awareness to purchase journey, but what does that actually allow you to do? What type of insights can you gain from that?\n\nCory Van Auken (33:16): So that gives us the full picture at a quick snapshot. Now our reports go way farther in depth on pieces of coverage. And like we said, we gather tons of analytics and behavior analytics and whatnot, but it allows us to say, here's the coverage we got from this month. Here is the impact that this piece of coverage has. Here's how we can amplify it. Here's the audience that we're talking to. And so it's again, a quick snapshot but allows you to see or start to see the value of each piece of coverage that we get. But it's by no means the only piece of analysis that we do with our reporting. So yeah, we go a lot farther in depth for a lot of these things.\n\nCole Heilborn (34:05): So how do you connect that to a value? Because value inherently is dollars and cents in its most conventional form, but if you're seeing some plots on a graph of where your PR hits are landing, how do you translate that to value? Or are there other ways that you look at value or consider value?", "token_estimate": 354, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Cory Van Auken"], "start_timestamp": "32:53", "end_timestamp": "34:05", "podcast_index": 51, "episode_name": "Ep 51: Cory Van Auken | Digital Marketing and PR | Darby Communications", "slug": "ep-51", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-51", "guest_name": "Cory Van Auken", "guest_title": "Darby Communications", "categories": [], "short_description": "We go deep inside Darby's approach to analyze how they integrate digital and PR", "podcast_length": "44:73"}, {"id": "31526379-870c-4e42-8bbe-5d5d3c29690e", "episode_id": "58fd0630-891d-4c4d-a89d-96aad5a6ae04", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4856", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Cory Van Auken (34:25): Yeah, so it completely depends on what your constituting value in this. And so in some ways I think what we're talking about in value here is very specifically conversions and sales. But I think we have to think about value for each funnel level, (34:44): And they don't necessarily all have the same return on investment depending on the value that you're using. So for bottom line impact, I think when you look at the value that a piece of coverage brings or the return on investment that you're getting, sales is a great sales or conversion numbers or goals is a great way to look at the value there. When you look at the digital impact, oftentimes a great value to look at is how can we do less with more? So we just got a lot of backlinks and we're helping your SEO. Well, you don't have to spend as much money\n\nSpeaker 3 (35:23): On\n\nCory Van Auken (35:25): Doing all this SEO work, or let's look at the brand awareness side of things. Well, this is a really big publication, we're getting a lot of eyes on your product here. Maybe it's worth spending less money on top funnel Facebook ads that you were just not seeing a lot of traction from and you're spending, they're getting increasingly more expensive and you're not seeing a return on ad spend there. So I think it depends on how the value level on how you determine that\n\nCole Heilborn (35:57): Is then the idea that you can take this for an individual piece of coverage, analyze it, and then can you take all of this, let's say for a client for a year and then look at where their entire PR journey for the last year was and then see what type of coverage you got and then start to quantify all the value behind that?", "token_estimate": 427, "speakers": ["Cory Van Auken", "Speaker 3", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "34:25", "end_timestamp": "35:57", "podcast_index": 51, "episode_name": "Ep 51: Cory Van Auken | Digital Marketing and PR | Darby Communications", "slug": "ep-51", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-51", "guest_name": "Cory Van Auken", "guest_title": "Darby Communications", "categories": [], "short_description": "We go deep inside Darby's approach to analyze how they integrate digital and PR", "podcast_length": "44:73"}, {"id": "e138b49c-90ed-49cc-a25a-a9e948f295e7", "episode_id": "58fd0630-891d-4c4d-a89d-96aad5a6ae04", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4856", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Cory Van Auken (36:19): Yeah, so that's a great question. There's two ways that I think we use this information and one is internally and one is externally. So I'll give an example with one of our clients. They're named Tapestry and they're a local company here, a husband and wife that make these awesome combinations of tapestry and tarps. So there's these really beautiful tarps that are used for festivals and eating outside, and they're super durable. I have one in my car that I use when I take the dogs on a hike, and they're awesome. And we started working with them at the beginning of 2020 and I think you can probably see where this is going, but their big market and their marketing plan was to spend time road tripping around festivals and talking with people so that they could see the product, and that's where they see most of their success. (37:10): Well, as you can imagine, they had this huge plan starting in February throughout the year to road trip, and that got shut down as soon as arts rolled around. And so all of a sudden they have to become really heavily invested in as an e-commerce business, which they didn't do a whole lot of e-commerce before. So what we tried to do is we took a look at the coverage that we had received prior, and we looked at the audiences that they were targeting, and we then took a look at the behavior of those audiences and what they did. We looked at their own media and how they were talking about their product. We looked at their social media and again how they were presenting themselves, and we found areas that we could market tapestry as not just a festival tarp. This is so much more than that. (38:19): And so my colleague Hannah, who is kind of in charge of the account, is really wonderful and adept at being able to take this information and start putting together a PR plan that facilitates this journey. And so what we saw was that as we started to use this information, so we looked at demographics of people and where these demographics matched other demographics. So like soccer moms were a great example of, hey, there's some overlap here in terms of the age, the demographics, income level. And we looked at publications that targeted those kind of folks and we put this in play and they've had a really successful 2020. We were able to replace all of their projected profit that they were going to get from go round festivals, and they replaced that with e-commerce success, (39:12): And they were able to spend less money than they thought on Facebook ads because we're bringing the right people, which means that we can retarget them easier. We successfully revised their email marketing strategy and their drip campaigns to be more efficient. And so all of these worked together to bolster their e-commerce success, and that was on the back of a lot of their PR success. And then we amplified that afterwards across their own channels and their social media and whatnot. And a lot of that data came from analyzing the previous year that we had and across all of our other accounts as well.", "token_estimate": 768, "speakers": ["Cory Van Auken"], "start_timestamp": "36:19", "end_timestamp": "36:19", "podcast_index": 51, "episode_name": "Ep 51: Cory Van Auken | Digital Marketing and PR | Darby Communications", "slug": "ep-51", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-51", "guest_name": "Cory Van Auken", "guest_title": "Darby Communications", "categories": [], "short_description": "We go deep inside Darby's approach to analyze how they integrate digital and PR", "podcast_length": "44:73"}, {"id": "3fac6734-c0d7-4206-8be7-b3b4e939677c", "episode_id": "58fd0630-891d-4c4d-a89d-96aad5a6ae04", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4856", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Cole Heilborn (39:55): So long story short, PR is no longer just pr, it's a ton of other stuff all connected together. And it sounds like that's kind of what you guys have learned, have had to learn to adapt to is learning how it all connects.\n\nCory Van Auken (40:11): And that's the hope is that it's connected seamlessly and so good PR works with good marketing to most of the whole brand up. And so there's some really cool ways that brands are figuring out how to incorporate PR into what they're doing. And we have a brand that's looking at the amount of money they spent on photo shoots for their product catalogs, and they were looking at ways to be more efficient. And one thing that we come up with together is why don't we pair the planning that we're doing for a PR event or a press jacket where we're going to some destination, we're inviting media there, we're showcasing the product in an environment that is suitable for the product and it's a wonderful experience. Well, this is a photogenic opportunity and we could save a bunch of money if we also paired this with a marketing photo shoot. And those two things weren't separate. And so that's one way that we can incorporate kind of what we're doing with what the marketing department's doing. (41:26): Another way we've done this is with product launches. And so with one of our brands, they were launching a whole new line of product and this was really special and it was our job to plan a media event that showcased this. However, the media event was two weeks or three weeks prior to the official launch. We worked with the marketing team to ensure that we had all of the assets we need share with the media that we were inviting down to this location to showcase it. We told all of the media we had to get buy-in from everybody that was coming saying, please don't talk about anything that you've seen here until this date. Once this date hits, please talk about it nonstop. And we had to get buy-in that way. And luckily the product was amazing enough. The experience was great that all of the media were bought in and agreed to that, and we were able to then treat all of the coverage there as part of the marketing campaign. And we were analyze it as a campaigning of itself to show the return on investment. And what we were able to do is show the direct impact of it too monetarily on their bottom line, which was really special. And we worked directly with the marketing team to do that, which was really cool. So it helped one that their marketing team was completely organized. We had everything we needed to ensure that all of the media that we invited to this event had the full lowdown of why this product launch was so special. (42:58): And then they were able to amplify the launch because we had all of a sudden a wave of coverage and they had this awesome experience and we had all of the assets for them already. We had a photographer there with us and we captured video. And so it was a really cool, for lack of a better term, synergy that we saw there, and it made for a really successful launch. And it wasn't just due to PR and it wasn't just due to marketing. And so it was cool to watch them work together. So that's another example of how this all works together.", "token_estimate": 815, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Cory Van Auken"], "start_timestamp": "39:55", "end_timestamp": "40:11", "podcast_index": 51, "episode_name": "Ep 51: Cory Van Auken | Digital Marketing and PR | Darby Communications", "slug": "ep-51", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-51", "guest_name": "Cory Van Auken", "guest_title": "Darby Communications", "categories": [], "short_description": "We go deep inside Darby's approach to analyze how they integrate digital and PR", "podcast_length": "44:73"}, {"id": "57f92d25-221b-4d5d-8f44-1fc1347246fa", "episode_id": "58fd0630-891d-4c4d-a89d-96aad5a6ae04", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4856", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Cole Heilborn (43:34): Yeah, no, that's awesome. So we're running out of time for today. One of my last few questions for you though is what areas either for you personally, for Darby, or just for the world of pr, do you feel like you have the most that you can improve on? And what are some of those pieces?\n\nCory Van Auken (43:56): That's a great question. I think there's always room to improve, to showcase that every brand needs a give back now and needs a purpose. And with PR is the same. We don't want to just be pushing product for pushing product's sake. I think where we can improve PR in general, and I heard the statistic, I'm going to quote it, I dunno how real it is, but I think the idea is right, that there's something like 10 PR practitioners for every writer out there that's writing about gear. And so there's a lot of PR in the world and it can be done very wrong where it's just spray and pray, just don't personalize everything. And I think that hurts PR in general. And so the idea where everyone can improve and darby com and PR as a whole is ensuring that we're connecting the product back to the story and the importance of the brand and why the brand's doing what it's doing. I think Om bro, you talked about was a great example. They're not just putting out really cool armless studies. There's a purpose for them and they're sequestering 330 times more carbon than they're producing. And I think that story is really important. So it's not just a sunglass that we're pushing out, there is a purpose to why they're doing what they're doing. So that's how I would answer that question.\n\nCole Heilborn (45:28): Okay. Yeah. Cool. And what about for you personally? What are you most excited to learn? What's your biggest personal challenge in the workplace that you're trying to wrap your head around?", "token_estimate": 457, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Cory Van Auken"], "start_timestamp": "43:34", "end_timestamp": "45:28", "podcast_index": 51, "episode_name": "Ep 51: Cory Van Auken | Digital Marketing and PR | Darby Communications", "slug": "ep-51", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-51", "guest_name": "Cory Van Auken", "guest_title": "Darby Communications", "categories": [], "short_description": "We go deep inside Darby's approach to analyze how they integrate digital and PR", "podcast_length": "44:73"}, {"id": "d285b087-3dfd-4ed2-a295-3eade73ca16a", "episode_id": "58fd0630-891d-4c4d-a89d-96aad5a6ae04", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4856", "chunk_index": 21, "content": "Cory Van Auken (45:39): Yeah, that's a great question. I think I just have so much to continue to learn. And I think there is, personally, my take on PR and marketing is I'm not sure you can master it because it's changing so quickly. And so I think the pursuit of mastery is important, not the fact that you can become a master. I'm not sure you can or I certainly don't feel like I am. So for me personally, it's continuing that pursuit and not feeling comfortable in what I know and how we're doing it, but continue to push it further. How can we do this better and how can we prove the return on investment and what we're doing and where is it that we can push the limits? So for me, it's just continuing to try to achieve that and knowing that I'll never get there.\n\nSpeaker 3 (46:31): I\n\nCory Van Auken (46:31): Have so much to learn. And in a lot of ways it's really cool to hear at the people that you have on your show and their journeys because I think everyone's had somewhat of a winding journey. I don't want to speak for everybody in the marketing realm and the outdoors, but like you said, it's aspirational. People get into it because first they were probably fans of the products they were using and want to get into it. So in some ways, I think we're all in the same boat then trying to tackle that and continue finding ways to push the boundaries of what's possible in what we're doing.\n\nCole Heilborn (47:07): Trying to be okay with the fact that you will never know everything is frustrating, but also it can be empowering once you get past that frustrating stage.", "token_estimate": 397, "speakers": ["Cory Van Auken", "Speaker 3", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "45:39", "end_timestamp": "47:07", "podcast_index": 51, "episode_name": "Ep 51: Cory Van Auken | Digital Marketing and PR | Darby Communications", "slug": "ep-51", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-51", "guest_name": "Cory Van Auken", "guest_title": "Darby Communications", "categories": [], "short_description": "We go deep inside Darby's approach to analyze how they integrate digital and PR", "podcast_length": "44:73"}, {"id": "541ac315-8e52-4414-8d42-b98b70f21d12", "episode_id": "58fd0630-891d-4c4d-a89d-96aad5a6ae04", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4856", "chunk_index": 22, "content": "Cory Van Auken (47:17): Yeah, I feel like I became a better coworker when I became more comfortable by saying, I don't know, and saying, that's a good question. I have no idea. Let me figure it out. Instead of trying to be somebody who could answer everything, there is so much that I don't know. And also being comfortable with failing a lot of ways too, and I think that's something that continue to learn and get comfortable with. So I don't know. I'm interested to hear your opinion on that with someone who's talked to so many really interesting marketers and PR practitioners in this field, and if you've heard answers that are pretty common throughout\n\nCole Heilborn (48:03): In terms of the personal challenges,\n\nCory Van Auken (48:06): Personal challenges and what's next. And I mean even for you as an agency owner, I feel like we have a lot in common in some ways in terms of that pursuit.\n\nCole Heilborn (48:21): So to be honest, I haven't asked a whole lot of guests that question. I think you're one of the first that I've specifically asked that question. I think those comments do get made throughout an interview with a guest. Let me think about that.\n\nCory Van Auken (48:40): Well, what I will say is that I think it's a great question and you'll probably get way better answers than you just received from me. But yeah, I think it's a great question.", "token_estimate": 343, "speakers": ["Cory Van Auken", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "47:17", "end_timestamp": "48:40", "podcast_index": 51, "episode_name": "Ep 51: Cory Van Auken | Digital Marketing and PR | Darby Communications", "slug": "ep-51", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-51", "guest_name": "Cory Van Auken", "guest_title": "Darby Communications", "categories": [], "short_description": "We go deep inside Darby's approach to analyze how they integrate digital and PR", "podcast_length": "44:73"}, {"id": "bdd6a93f-a982-4177-b5f4-53050a3842bd", "episode_id": "58fd0630-891d-4c4d-a89d-96aad5a6ae04", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4856", "chunk_index": 23, "content": "Cole Heilborn (48:50): Well, I guess maybe while I don't have any specific responses that I can recite, I think I can comment on just the attitude that I've picked up from everyone who's been on the show. I think the thing that I've walked away with the most is that, and maybe it's just specific to the people that I've chosen, because these are the type of people that I prefer in life. Maybe this is just indicative of the marketing industry or maybe the outdoor industry, but everyone is just really humble and really just calm and chill. No one is trying to be, at least if they are, they're really good at it, but no one seems to be trying to be bigger than who they are. Everyone is just happy to be doing what they're doing and realizing that there's some things they're doing well, some things they're learning at, some things they failed at. And I think that's what's been really cool is just to get a sense that everyone is just kind of content and still striving to do better, but really humble about the accomplishments that they've had or the failures that they've had. And I think that's been really cool and it's been exciting to be somewhat a part of because that gives me a lot of hope for just people in general.\n\nCory Van Auken (50:14): Totally. I totally feel that. And I think that's part of the reason why I love working at Darby communication so much is Coral, the founder has been doing this for quite a while and we have a really awesome team and everybody is okay with one asking for help. Two, understanding that there's a lot we don't know and we can continue to work on. And when the work atmosphere is built around that and the continued pursuit of getting better and not feeling like you have to be perfect, I think that's where really progressive really awesome ideas come out of. And it's just also pleasure to go to work and do that and feel like you're part of a team. So in some ways, I think you're totally right where the guests that you have had on the show and you and the podcast you've created is kind of this community that totally has that throughout, which also makes it easier to ask for help. Right?", "token_estimate": 537, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Cory Van Auken"], "start_timestamp": "48:50", "end_timestamp": "50:14", "podcast_index": 51, "episode_name": "Ep 51: Cory Van Auken | Digital Marketing and PR | Darby Communications", "slug": "ep-51", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-51", "guest_name": "Cory Van Auken", "guest_title": "Darby Communications", "categories": [], "short_description": "We go deep inside Darby's approach to analyze how they integrate digital and PR", "podcast_length": "44:73"}, {"id": "5390290f-ef4c-4ffa-a281-570960108fe5", "episode_id": "58fd0630-891d-4c4d-a89d-96aad5a6ae04", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4856", "chunk_index": 24, "content": "Cole Heilborn (51:18): Totally. Yeah. And that's been cool too, is just as the podcast has gotten going, I've had past guests or guests that I've recorded with reach out to me and say, Hey, I listened to so-and-so's interview. It really resonated with me. Can you connect us? And a lot of times people who have been on the show all know each other. You probably wouldn't surprise you, but the number of people who come onto the show and say, oh yeah, you interviewed 20 of my good buddies. That happens all the time. But sometimes there's folks who come on and they're like, man, I really resonated with so-and-so's episode. Can you connect us? I want to ask them questions like, yeah, that's awesome. That's what this is all about. So I hope that that sort of attitude can continue and that everyone who's a part of the show to this point and into the future is excited to just contribute to the overall growth of everyone in this space.\n\nCory Van Auken (52:11): Yeah, man, I totally agree, and I think what you're doing here, and honestly the questions you're asking is refreshing too, because it's all about learning. So totally agree.\n\nCole Heilborn (52:22): Well, it comes from a place of not knowing anything. So as long as I don't, I write that with you. As long as I don't learn at all, then hopefully the questions keep coming.\n\nCory Van Auken (52:33): Right.\n\nCole Heilborn (52:34): Well, Corey, thanks again for taking the time to chat today. If folks want to follow along with you personally or with the brand, where can people go to learn more?\n\nCory Van Auken (52:42): Yeah. Well, I personally am a bit of a Luddite and don't have, or I've gotten rid of a lot of my social media accounts, but I'm on LinkedIn, Corey Van Kin, but check out darby communications.com, check us out on Instagram. We have some fun stuff there on LinkedIn, Facebook, and Twitter.\n\nCole Heilborn (53:01): Cool. Also, you're not the very first person who is a marketer in the outdoor world who has gotten rid of most of their social media. I find that very interesting and maybe a potential topic in the future.\n\nCory Van Auken (53:13): Yeah, there's something to explore there. For sure.\n\nCole Heilborn (53:16): Alright, Corey, well have a good rest of your day and I wish you guys the best in 2021.\n\nCory Van Auken (53:21): Yeah, thanks Cole. I appreciate your time. This has been fun.\n\nCole Heilborn (53:24): Alright, take care. Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 658, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Cory Van Auken"], "start_timestamp": "51:18", "end_timestamp": "53:24", "podcast_index": 51, "episode_name": "Ep 51: Cory Van Auken | Digital Marketing and PR | Darby Communications", "slug": "ep-51", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-51", "guest_name": "Cory Van Auken", "guest_title": "Darby Communications", "categories": [], "short_description": "We go deep inside Darby's approach to analyze how they integrate digital and PR", "podcast_length": "44:73"}, {"id": "411c4309-db35-4e44-9efb-f7b39500b2b5", "episode_id": "d94c730e-2348-4344-99a7-bebeea1a4908", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4859", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Kansas Sartin (00:09): But also talking to customers is also how we learn pain points. Like what are they looking for? What are they trying to do, where are they going? And my absolute favorite thing is when customers send us pictures on their trips and stuff and how they uploaded it and what they're doing and where they're going and smiles and all that stuff. That's my absolute favorite thing to see.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:33): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing podcast. My name is Cole Hallborn and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Back Country Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Back Country Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Holborn. Today I'm sitting down with Kansas Sarton, the founder of Pac Mule, Kansas. How are you doing?\n\nKansas Sartin (01:15): Good, Cole. Thanks, man. Appreciate you having us on.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:18): Absolutely. Thanks for taking the time. Where are you calling in from today?\n\nKansas Sartin (01:22): We're in New Braunfels, Texas. We originated in Houston, and then as a lot of people have made an exodus from large cities during COVID, et cetera, I'd say that probably the moving point, at least for my wife in making that decision was my son fishing in a drainage ditch, which was really catching snapping turtles, and she was like, this poor kid, okay, I'm going to follow Kansas to someplace that has a lot more out outdoor activity. So we made the end of the summer, which has been great.", "token_estimate": 466, "speakers": ["Kansas Sartin", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:09", "end_timestamp": "01:22", "podcast_index": 52, "episode_name": "Ep 52: Kansas Sartin | Never Settle | PAKMULE Cargo Carrier", "slug": "ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "guest_name": "Kansas Sartin", "guest_title": "PAKMULE", "categories": [], "short_description": "Monster beatings and still keeps going", "podcast_length": "44:74"}, {"id": "4d563f2b-0ddb-46f2-af53-6c69e476d224", "episode_id": "d94c730e-2348-4344-99a7-bebeea1a4908", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4859", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:54): Oh, nice. Well, I can't, but admire, you got a very, I'm looking at your video here. Obviously folks can't see this, but you have an awesome color palette going on because you're in your shop, you've got this rad kind of light, dusty, blue jeep behind you, and your packed mule branded clothing is also the same color. Is that intentional?\n\nKansas Sartin (02:13): No, just luck of the draw. I guess. I can't say that I'm a real classy dresser or anything. We just grab a shirt and a hat and the Jeep is the teal blue is the official color that I found out at sema. We had a Jeep collector specialist in JP Magazine that came by and they told us a whole lot more about it than we ever knew, which was pretty fun. But yeah, that's Willie in the background. He was a driver in helping to get the idea of PAC meal off the ground and get excited about it. No cargo space.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:56): Sure. Yeah. No cargo space, but an open top.\n\nKansas Sartin (03:00): That's right. Yeah, that's right.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:01): Well, yeah, I'm excited to spend our time today learning more about the company, talking through what Pac Mule is, how you guys got started, but first I want to give a shout out to Rick over at Howler Bros for connecting us. By the time your episode comes out, Rick's will already be out. His is actually coming out this next week. Excuse me. So I guess I'm curious, how do you know Rick? What's your guys' relationship been like?", "token_estimate": 368, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Kansas Sartin"], "start_timestamp": "01:54", "end_timestamp": "03:01", "podcast_index": 52, "episode_name": "Ep 52: Kansas Sartin | Never Settle | PAKMULE Cargo Carrier", "slug": "ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "guest_name": "Kansas Sartin", "guest_title": "PAKMULE", "categories": [], "short_description": "Monster beatings and still keeps going", "podcast_length": "44:74"}, {"id": "d42e05bf-49f4-4c47-91ce-f909b6586f9c", "episode_id": "d94c730e-2348-4344-99a7-bebeea1a4908", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4859", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Kansas Sartin (03:28): Yeah, just through the industry really. And when Rick is one of the best humans that's walking on this planet, and he, as I was getting pne started and trying to understand marketing and branding and those sorts of things, just looking around and thinking, who are these other folks that are doing a good job? And there's so many great brands that are coming out of Texas right now. It's kind of exciting. And so I knew Rick had been at Yeti in the early days and had been real instrumental in that and then moved over to Howler and has really done a great job with their whole entire marketing effort and just super wise guy, really smart also. And so I honestly just reached out to him kind of on a whim and he was gracious enough to respond and we kind of chatted for a few minutes and said, Hey, next time I'm in Austin, I'd love to buy lunch or a drink or whatever and just kind of pick your brain on a few things. And so we just became friends through that. And so he's been a great year through the growth of Pac Meal for me to call randomly and pick his brain on a couple items. And hopefully this doesn't create a chain of people reaching out to Rick and overwhelming his life with folks looking for nuggets of wisdom. But we've become friends through the whole process. And now I'm just about 30 minutes down the road, so it's good to be close.\n\nCole Heilborn (05:01): Well, he certainly has a number of nuggets of wisdom to share, and if folks are interested, I'd recommend you go check out that episode. We talked a lot about branding and branding specifically during pandemic and what Howler Bros has been up to in 2020. By the time this episode comes out, it'll be in 2021, but if you want to relive 2020, go take a listen to Rick's episode. But Kansas, I'm curious to learn a little bit more about Pac Mule. Before we dive into the story though, could you brief us on what you guys produce and how you got started?", "token_estimate": 488, "speakers": ["Kansas Sartin", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "03:28", "end_timestamp": "05:01", "podcast_index": 52, "episode_name": "Ep 52: Kansas Sartin | Never Settle | PAKMULE Cargo Carrier", "slug": "ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "guest_name": "Kansas Sartin", "guest_title": "PAKMULE", "categories": [], "short_description": "Monster beatings and still keeps going", "podcast_length": "44:74"}, {"id": "016543ba-91f5-4fff-8cf1-a7eb1f4a8dfc", "episode_id": "d94c730e-2348-4344-99a7-bebeea1a4908", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4859", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Kansas Sartin (05:37): Yeah. We make aluminum hitch-mounted cargo carriers. The hitch mounted cargo racks. Cargo carriers have been around for 40 years and you can find 60 different units on the market by a dozen different manufacturers, maybe more than a dozen at this point, but they're all kind of the same. And honestly, there's a handful of ones that folks are familiar with. They're all manufactured by the same person. They're just sort of white labeled for these different box stores and none of 'em are real exciting. They don't do anything real well. And what I mean is that you're adding a cargo carrier because you need space, but a lot of them are pretty small and even the larger ones aren't all that big. And so you put one cooler and that's about all that it'll hold, which isn't necessarily a lot of space. And then the secondary sort of negative is that when you put that cooler on, you now can't drop your tailgate or raise a lift gate to your SUV. (06:37): And so what I was finding is that I had lift gates on my SUVs and I'm constantly carrying a dog with me, whether it's for retrieving or just for having my dog. He is a part of the family at this point. And you'd end up somewhere and you'd have to take the stuff off the cargo carrier so you could raise the lift gate so you could get your dog in and out. And I have very little patience for stuff that doesn't make a lot of sense. And so I'll spend a ridiculous amount of time and money trying to make something work really great, probably more time than it would ever take me to take that cooler off. And so through that pain point, I'd been thinking about how does this change and how does this become better? And there's some other storyline there about sort of that one moment of the steel one that my daughter cut her forehead on it, and then I tried to take it off and I dropped it on my foot and it was that like, oh my God, there has got to be a better way. (07:41): This is so ridiculous. And the steel ones are resting and there's no way to store them, and they're leaning up against your fence. And so a couple weeks later, I'm on my boat chasing redfish and sort of that sunset cruise beer drinking program on the way back into the harbor and holding onto the windshield grab rail. And I thought, well, that's it. And I'm kind of looking around the boat and all these boats on the Texas coast and in Florida of course as well. And on the East coast, well, any coastline really, but thinking more in the shallow water back bay boats, you've got these T tops and polling platforms and casting decks, and they're all made out of a bright aluminum. They're TIG welded, they look great, they don't rust, and they're strong enough where people can stand on 'em and cast from 'em and all that. And I started thinking, well, that's it. (08:30): And nobody was doing anything with it. And so I just laid it out. Honestly, the first pack mule wasn't a pack mule, it was just Kansas needs a better way to haul gear. And so I just made one straight up. I wanted to hold, this is still in the early days when Yetis were just starting to become, I mean people were aware of them, but it was still like, really? Do you spend three 50 on a cooler? And I had put a couple on my boat, which was kind of the inception for those coolers, and I realized, man, these things are great. They could be a tabletop on the back, you can cut on 'em, it's a bar. They're super useful to having a hitch carrier on the back. And so I wanted to hold 2 65 side by side and still be able to raise my lift gate so I could get my dog in and out of the back. (09:18): And everything's piped. So you can use our cambuckle mule straps, which allows you to secure the Rotomolded coolers underneath the lid. So you walk back there, you just open the lid, you don't have to unstrap anything. So again, it's about I don't have patience for things that don't work. When I get to my destination point, I want to open a cooler and grab a beer right away. I don't want to have to undo a ratchet strap or something. I better have easy access to that stuff. And so that was really kind of the driving point and just my being OCD with gear, trying to make it easy to load gear, easy to access it all because everybody's had that pain point in the field wearing gear that just isn't quite up to par. It's an off-brand, it's maybe made in China or whatever, just another stamped out piece of equipment. (10:09): And you're miserable because either your feet hurt or you've got blisters or you're wet or you're cold or it's not doing what you really wish that it was doing or maybe what you thought it was going to do. And so that time that you've taken away from friends, family, the money you've spent on the travel, vacation time from work, and then you're kind of wishing that either you were already done with your height because your feet hurt so much, or you were in a hot shower drinking some warm coffee, so cold, or you wish you were back getting dried off. And so you don't get to engage in that experience. And so really the purpose of Pac Mule is to support people, make it easy to encourage them to load up, go see America, the wild spaces that exist. Go learn something, try something new, don't be intimidated by it. And then when you're there, we don't want you to even think about Pac Mule or how your gear is. All your gear goes in its place. It's easy. You can find it so that all your time and efforts focus on that place and the people you're with.", "token_estimate": 1377, "speakers": ["Kansas Sartin"], "start_timestamp": "05:37", "end_timestamp": "05:37", "podcast_index": 52, "episode_name": "Ep 52: Kansas Sartin | Never Settle | PAKMULE Cargo Carrier", "slug": "ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "guest_name": "Kansas Sartin", "guest_title": "PAKMULE", "categories": [], "short_description": "Monster beatings and still keeps going", "podcast_length": "44:74"}, {"id": "99e93709-204d-4eb3-89e8-e204678b4df9", "episode_id": "d94c730e-2348-4344-99a7-bebeea1a4908", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4859", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Cole Heilborn (11:16): Yeah, man. Well, no, I think we've all been in those environments where we're going out on some adventure or whatever, and we packed that piece of gear. That's not ideal, but it was maybe half off or it was a little cheaper than the other options. And then you get out in the field and you're going out there, you're like, oh, it can't be that bad. Think about how much money I just saved. And then you get out there and you're like, oh man, I realized why this thing was half as much as the alternative option. And you quickly regret your choice, and then you end up going back and spending spending money on the full price item, and now you've just spent one and a half times as\n\nKansas Sartin (11:56): Much,\n\nCole Heilborn (11:57): Whereas if you just paid the price for the quality piece to begin with. So I feel like everyone who's been outside and done something can probably resonate with that story just a little bit.", "token_estimate": 237, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Kansas Sartin"], "start_timestamp": "11:16", "end_timestamp": "11:57", "podcast_index": 52, "episode_name": "Ep 52: Kansas Sartin | Never Settle | PAKMULE Cargo Carrier", "slug": "ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "guest_name": "Kansas Sartin", "guest_title": "PAKMULE", "categories": [], "short_description": "Monster beatings and still keeps going", "podcast_length": "44:74"}, {"id": "286e247b-c6b3-43db-8e26-c307ec823f7b", "episode_id": "d94c730e-2348-4344-99a7-bebeea1a4908", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4859", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Kansas Sartin (12:08): Yeah, my wife is really the one that drew that to my attention. She said, golly, you keep buying this crappy stuff. And when you're young and you first get married and you're first working and stuff, you get into something, you don't have a lot of money to spend on things. But she saw that I was, I'd go out and buy a pair of snake boots and then they were miserable and my feet hurt and I was getting blisters. And she's like, do you even know if that'll protect you from a rattlesnake if one hits? And you're like, I don't really know. That's not a good sign. And so she said exactly what you did. Just wait. Just wait a little bit longer. Use what you've got and save up and get the right thing. Because spending more money, you eventually buy the high quality piece and then you love it and you have it forever and you use it, and then you have memories. And now you put on a pair of sims waiters that you've been fishing or duck hunting out of for six, seven years, and they're still perfect, and you have all the memories of 'em. Or you put on those old pair of snake boots and it's like, man, how many miles have I walked in these boots? And they're still working good, looking good, and you feel safe and confident, and there's a lot. And so her coin term that I've heard some other folks say too is just buy once.\n\nCole Heilborn (13:28): I like that. I've never heard that before. Maybe that hasn't made its way up to Washington yet. Maybe that's just a Texas thing maybe. So how long ago did you have that realization to start Pac Meal? What's been the journey? What has been the journey with starting the company and where are you guys at today?", "token_estimate": 420, "speakers": ["Kansas Sartin", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "12:08", "end_timestamp": "13:28", "podcast_index": 52, "episode_name": "Ep 52: Kansas Sartin | Never Settle | PAKMULE Cargo Carrier", "slug": "ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "guest_name": "Kansas Sartin", "guest_title": "PAKMULE", "categories": [], "short_description": "Monster beatings and still keeps going", "podcast_length": "44:74"}, {"id": "f95d4f35-d047-4dd1-88aa-280e49355293", "episode_id": "d94c730e-2348-4344-99a7-bebeea1a4908", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4859", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Kansas Sartin (13:47): Yeah, it's been wild and crazy unlike anything I probably would've thought it would be. It's easy to have an idea and either A, be completely overwhelmed with all the steps that it takes to get there, or B, think, well, it's going to be pretty easy. And then as it is, in my case, four years later, you're like, whoa, we've really learned a lot here. And so the first pack mule, the one that wasn't a pack mule, it was just my own product. That was probably eight or so years ago that I built that and people were stopping me on the road all the time, where'd you get that? I got to have it. I'd be at a gas station, raise the lift gate to take the dog out to go, let 'em use the bathroom and the grass, and I'd have all this stuff on the back. (14:38): And they're like, wait, how'd you do that? You didn't have to pull a pen or move anything or use a swing arm or whatever. It's like, no, you pack it right? And it's designed this way. And so my wife and I talked about it and I said, golly, I'm not going to quit my job and just go do this thing. I'm going to keep, we have kids and mortgage and all the other things, but I'll double down on this. And I've got a pretty big tinkering intuition. I was always doing something outside of nine to five anyways. And so the idea was, look, if we can do this responsibly, if it can not just be something else that people feel like they have to buy, but if we're really making an impact on people, if we can encourage people to go outside where they can find that place to exhale, and then in turn they appreciate it, so they want to help conserve it, then I feel like we're doing something right. (15:36): And so February will be four years that we've had product available to sell. And the first three years of that, I was running a construction business for some other people and moonlighting working until one or two o'clock in the morning each night doing stuff. And then on Fridays I'd fly or drive somewhere for a trade show and then be home at one or two in the morning and then back in the office the next day and just doing whatever you could to try to grow it, but also keep paying the bills. And so I think a lot of people have this idea, I'm going to do this thing and I'm going to quit my job and it's suddenly going to work. And I had a doctor that he said, yeah, I opened my office. I go to med school. He was a Harvard medical degree or whatever, or maybe Harvard was an undergrad. I don't know where he went to medical school, but he said, for two weeks I have this office that's open and nobody's coming to me. He said, where are all the patients? And that's the hardest thing. It's like you think you have this thing available. And then through trade shows and stuff, that still our biggest challenge is letting people know that we exist and how do you do that? And that atmosphere is constantly changing. (16:53): Used to be print ad and billboards and mailers. And now of course, digital ads are more the driver. And so you're constantly trying to figure out how to make people aware. And so year one was really kind of a, let's make these available. Let's make a few hundred of 'em and see if people will buy 'em and what they think about it. And towards the end of year one, we made the sway back model, which is the lower rail for Jeeps, like the newer model Jeeps with the spare tire on the back. So it would slide underneath the spare tire, and you still have access and no extensions or anything. And that's been a great market for us because Jeeps have no cargo space inherently. And so you can enjoy the Jeep top down doors off wherever you want to go and whatever you want to do, it's easy to park. (17:42): And then when you need to go to the hardware store or you need extra space for a road trip when you're going somewhere, you've got that cargo space. And some people bring up ground clearance and some of those things. And we do have a ridge line model that has extra departure angle vis-a-vis an eight inch riser. But really the design isn't you're going to go climb Moab in this thing. The design is really, it helps you get to your destination. You can take it off at that place. It only takes a minute and then go do your trail riding and stuff, leave it at your campsite if you want to. I drag mine everywhere, and I've run it on some crazy trails, and I've probably hit rocks a handful of times without damage and I don't really worry about it. But I'm also, again, not doing crazy Moab stuff. It's a little more tame, I", "token_estimate": 1133, "speakers": ["Kansas Sartin"], "start_timestamp": "13:47", "end_timestamp": "13:47", "podcast_index": 52, "episode_name": "Ep 52: Kansas Sartin | Never Settle | PAKMULE Cargo Carrier", "slug": "ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "guest_name": "Kansas Sartin", "guest_title": "PAKMULE", "categories": [], "short_description": "Monster beatings and still keeps going", "podcast_length": "44:74"}, {"id": "4a61cb8c-e5c3-4ded-92b3-803aac0eeb24", "episode_id": "d94c730e-2348-4344-99a7-bebeea1a4908", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4859", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole Heilborn (18:34): Think. Yeah. Yeah. So that's awesome. Four years in, and as the company has grown, has the team grown, is it still just you moonlighting or?\n\nKansas Sartin (18:46): No, I quit my job September of 2019 and have just been fully focused on Pac Mule, which of course that's a scary thing too. You know that it's growing, but you really have to be a hundred percent focused on it and give the 80 hours just to this instead of doing 40 at night. And so it's great and it's exciting. And then COVID hits and nobody can travel anywhere. Nobody is spending money, we're not sure about jobs. And then you start second guessing a little bit like, whoa, is this the right thing to do? And I say, second guessing, you always second guess everything that you do to some degree, but still very confident in the business and what we're doing and the products we're making and what we're all about. And so this has been a tremendous year, and sales have been great and just we're working on a lot of new products, accessories that'll mount onto the Pac Mule and how you haul other things on it and make that more viable. (19:52): And then what other things fit within the portfolio of Pac Mule? And so of course we have a laundry list of things we'd love to do that's a mile long, and just trying to figure out which ones really make sense in a broader market. And then which ones first is the other piece of it, it's me and my wife. I have contracted most of our other labor. And so being in Houston in an oil and gas market, I've been through the 2008 recession where oil tanked and all the jobs shut down and people were getting laid off and it was crazy. And then managed the recent construction business through the 2016 downturn in Houston and going through layoffs and closing business units and all that stuff. And what I learned through all that is you have to be as light, nimble, lean, and variable as possible. (20:49): So if something happens, you can turn off some light switches real fast and it's not a major impact to you or your business or whatever else. And so we just try to keep it lean and people are always surprised by how lean we are. And even my accountant's like, Hey, listen, there's nothing else you can do to get leaner. I mean, how do you even eat? And I'm like, I get a couple cliff bars and a can of soup when I'm on the road and I'll try to go light. I don't need to too much to keep it going. And then just by nature of it all, I mean I don't very rarely stay in a hotel or an Airbnb. I'm usually camping somewhere. And I recently plugged a four wheel camper in the bed of the truck, which upgraded my stuff a little bit more from my rooftop tent program, but I prefer to be there, a hotel room, you got to bring all your stuff in or you're not sure if somebody's going to break into your car in the parking lot, and people are slamming doors all night. (21:52): It's just never a real good sleep. And I prefer to see the countryside, so I map out where the state parks are, and I've had park rangers wake me up on a cot outside of the station when they've had the gate closing. I got there at midnight and I'll just sleep outside underneath the stars. I love it. And it seems kind of a funny thing maybe for somebody like me to be doing, but it's what I love to do. And I think it also is how I'm able to continue to innovate in new products and how the company continues to see problems and source and fix 'em.", "token_estimate": 864, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Kansas Sartin"], "start_timestamp": "18:34", "end_timestamp": "18:46", "podcast_index": 52, "episode_name": "Ep 52: Kansas Sartin | Never Settle | PAKMULE Cargo Carrier", "slug": "ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "guest_name": "Kansas Sartin", "guest_title": "PAKMULE", "categories": [], "short_description": "Monster beatings and still keeps going", "podcast_length": "44:74"}, {"id": "4a12a623-fd9f-40a0-a5f9-e489cda2befa", "episode_id": "d94c730e-2348-4344-99a7-bebeea1a4908", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4859", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (22:30): Yeah, no, that's great. I'm glad to hear that the work hasn't gotten in the way of the things that you love to do outside. Sometimes that happens accidentally. Yeah. So you mentioned that this year has been pretty crazy just in terms of sales production challenges, what's been happening?\n\nKansas Sartin (22:54): No, production's been fantastic At the shop. We plugged in a no visitors policy so that we could keep the workers safe and just keep everything manufacturing. So when everybody else that import stuff had to shut down, we were able to keep providing product to folks. And I would say March, April, when travel got restricted, that got scary because now nobody needs a pack meal to load to go nowhere. And so that was kind of unnerving. But then through the summer, southern sales were fantastic because people were driving and we were spending more money on ads and letting people know we exist. And there was some concern about, well, we're not doing trade shows, these different trade shows that we meet a lot of folks.\n\nCole Heilborn (23:45): So yeah. Tell me a little bit about, well, I guess I'm curious, are those products that you can talk about, what do you guys have coming out? Is that public knowledge yet?", "token_estimate": 313, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Kansas Sartin"], "start_timestamp": "22:30", "end_timestamp": "23:45", "podcast_index": 52, "episode_name": "Ep 52: Kansas Sartin | Never Settle | PAKMULE Cargo Carrier", "slug": "ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "guest_name": "Kansas Sartin", "guest_title": "PAKMULE", "categories": [], "short_description": "Monster beatings and still keeps going", "podcast_length": "44:74"}, {"id": "fae2a64a-be42-454f-b05e-912f43c7fe83", "episode_id": "d94c730e-2348-4344-99a7-bebeea1a4908", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4859", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Kansas Sartin (23:56): Well, the ones that we're getting ready to release we can talk about. So sort of basic things. A light kit for instance, it's not every day that people ask us about a black pack mule, we make 'em out of aluminum. It's a bright aluminum, like the same series, 6,000 series aluminum that they make horse trailers out of. So it's already naturally bright without having to anodize. It gives good reflectivity, it stands out apart from your vehicle. I think it looks good, but there are always customers that are like, man, I'd really love to have it black. Is that an option? Et cetera. And until we had a light kit in a way to provide some reflectivity and some awareness of the product being there, I just wasn't something we can consider. So we've got a light kit we're going to release that I'm super amped up about, it's integrated. (24:47): So clean brake turns signals, just a running light, and then all the wires get integrated through a piece of decking. So there's no way that brush, if you're driving off road, is going to snag the wires and rip it and then it's all, you can pull it all apart. And LED bulbs that are supposed to last 10 years made by Grody in the US who's a company that's been around for forever. Actually, the lenses on my old Jeep back there are grody lenses from 1980. So, but if a bulb were to ever go out or something crushed, you can pull it apart and replace it and you don't have to buy the whole thing over again, which was real important to me. And then a step to get up onto the Pack Mule. So if you've got a raised vehicle or if you've got a tailgate down and you're trying to step up and get up into the tailgate and it's kind of in your way if you're loading a kayak up onto an SUV, it makes it easier to get up onto your pack mule so that you can get up there. (25:44): But really the step is more focused or the driver was the four wheel camper slide in truck camper customer who has a camper in the bed of their truck, or maybe they've got rooftop tents in the bed of their truck, a bunch of stuff that's there in the bed and they need to get up and get into it. And so they're climbing up and down a lot. And then decking where you can actually create a little bit of a floor on the Pack Mule, so it's easier to walk onto it, but it's spaced enough where still all the pipe is a tie down spot. You've got enough gap where you can still run our Pack Mule webbing through there and strap it down with our cam buckles. (26:25): And then we've got all the stuff for next year is a little bit quiet, but just think about any sort of outdoor activity or sport that's fun and adventurous and the products you use for that. And then how do you carry 'em on a pack Mule is sort of the idea there. Yeah, it is pretty fun and exciting and I look forward to getting through it. They all seem to become more complicated with the variety of things that are out there, and I really try hard with the products that we develop to make them as universal as possible. I hate to have to have 10 skews for something depending on what you've got. And then you change whatever you've got and now you got to go buy a different one. I want it to be as universal as possible. And that's the way that the Pack Mule is. (27:15): The original one fits 95% of vehicles. The only thing it doesn't really work on is a Jeep with a spare tire on the back. If you've got barn doors like on a Sprinter van, you need the Swayback model with the lower rail. And then if you've got a 200 series lane cruiser with the tailgate that drops, the Swayback model works better. And then the Swayback works on a hundred percent of vehicles. So it's good to have units that are back to my OCD, I want them to work on all the vehicles. I want people to not have to make a bunch of modifications and cut stuff. And you can see all sorts of YouTube videos for the other hitch carriers out there that people are like, Hey, so I've made it work and all I had to do was cut this off and bend this and move it out of the way. And man, that's not right. That means that somebody didn't do the design work correctly.", "token_estimate": 1032, "speakers": ["Kansas Sartin"], "start_timestamp": "23:56", "end_timestamp": "23:56", "podcast_index": 52, "episode_name": "Ep 52: Kansas Sartin | Never Settle | PAKMULE Cargo Carrier", "slug": "ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "guest_name": "Kansas Sartin", "guest_title": "PAKMULE", "categories": [], "short_description": "Monster beatings and still keeps going", "podcast_length": "44:74"}, {"id": "9b9ae800-5024-467e-b12f-2ac1258163e2", "episode_id": "d94c730e-2348-4344-99a7-bebeea1a4908", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4859", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Cole Heilborn (28:09): Right, right. Yeah. So you mentioned you guys have been doing some advertising, some marketing. I'm always curious how, I mean, everyone is thinking about marketing. What are you guys doing? What have you tried? I mean, as you said, you can start a business, you get that LLC license and you have product, but that doesn't necessarily mean you are in business because no one is buying your product. So how have you gotten people to be interested in it? Obviously you had a bunch of word of mouth buzz just from the beginning, but I guess how have you kind of grown your sales and done some marketing along the way?", "token_estimate": 160, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "28:09", "end_timestamp": "28:09", "podcast_index": 52, "episode_name": "Ep 52: Kansas Sartin | Never Settle | PAKMULE Cargo Carrier", "slug": "ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "guest_name": "Kansas Sartin", "guest_title": "PAKMULE", "categories": [], "short_description": "Monster beatings and still keeps going", "podcast_length": "44:74"}, {"id": "05d12e90-9926-4002-b086-a68b7afaf92d", "episode_id": "d94c730e-2348-4344-99a7-bebeea1a4908", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4859", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Kansas Sartin (28:52): Year over year, our growth has been tremendous. This year we'll be extensively so just because of our digital marketing efforts, and we tried different groups that said they know what they're doing and have found that they don't necessarily. And so we found a group that was really, really sharp and has done a good job with that and has been able to find the right customers that would be interested in a pack meal. And so that's the digital marketing thing. That's really a certain group of people that understand how to turn the knobs and pull the levers. Otherwise, I think a lot of people comment to us, and I'm just a person, so for me, people saying these things, it's interesting, but they say, man, y'all are so innovative and y'all are doing such a great job with your marketing and your logos and all of your swag is cool and the new products you're coming out with, which makes me excited. (29:54): I mean, I love to hear people say that, and I'm kind of a nose to the grindstone. It's like, alright, we got that product done. What's the next thing that we're doing? I might have a glass of whiskey meat as a celebration that night and then that's it. I'm back to work. There's not a lot of hoorahs for me, and maybe it's just my nature, maybe it's the construction background, it's just get work, work, work, get it done. But I'm a work hard, play hard. So when I check out, man, I am off grid and exploring and trying to see and do everything, and I integrate that into what we do. And that's what I love about Pac Meal is that to test products and to innovate new products, I have to be out there in the field. And we're different in that I have product designers that help us think through touch, feel, and put it into cad, but a lot of it is me prototyping stuff here in the shop and just knocking around and trying to see what works and what doesn't before I ever start drafting in any sort of a CAD model. (31:02): And so we want to improve our hat selections and we want to do some fun shirts and we want to expand in that where people can be a part of the brand more than just having a pack mule and how can they engage the brand? And there's some different things that we want to do with that. The videos I think have been really helpful for people. When people get to the website, our conversions have been better. We did some intro videos explaining what Pack Mule's all about and how it works and showing how easy it is to interact with and around the Pack Mule. And so I think when people get to the website and they're able to see those, I think that helps a lot. And so we're just also trying to be real about it. We're not, I think that companies that just send out the design work and the product development work, I think they miss a lot. (32:01): And we want to hit the bullseye each time. And Bulletproof, I mean, there can always be something, but by design, PNE is going to take a monster beating and keep going. I'm hard on my stuff. If it's four wheel drive, it's going to go through some rowdy terrain. If it's a shallow water boat, I'm probably going to park it on a sandbar somewhere at some point and have to try to pull it out. And so having stuff that's durable and can take whatever abuse somebody puts on it, if they go to a feed store and they put 700 pounds of feed on the back, I want to know that they're going to get that feed to their ranch and not be scared that they're a hundred or 200 or 300 pounds over. And so we have a two and a half X safety factor built into the pack meal to account for the dynamic load. (32:58): And we have so many customers that come to us and say, Hey, we had a hitch carrier. It said it had a 500 pound capacity, we had two or 300 pounds on the back, and it's all bent already. And it's so many of those, and I don't know how they do this or why they even get away with it, but they have a static load rating on a hitch carrier that's meant to be on a vehicle that's got a dynamic load going down the highway or a trail or something. And so it doesn't take a lot to (33:24): Overstress those products. And so we're an expensive product and I get that. And fact Neal's not for everybody for sure, but it definitely fits a need for a lot of people that are looking for an easier place to load their gear, an easier way to access it all and demand more out of the products that they use. And so we're proud of what we're doing and just the sustainability piece of it too, we're 70% or more recycled content in our products. So we have huge scraps of metal. We melt 'em down, we pour it into billet, we extrude all the aluminum shapes, bend them, cut it, and then weld it, and then we temper it for eight hours at 350 degrees. And so it's a serious process to build it, but every time you weld aluminum, you actually weaken it a little bit. And so we're the only cargo carrier on the market that is actually welding and then tempering afterwards. And that's very unique to us, and it's part of the reason that we're so substantially strong in our design and in our fatigue stresses and all those things.", "token_estimate": 1277, "speakers": ["Kansas Sartin"], "start_timestamp": "28:52", "end_timestamp": "28:52", "podcast_index": 52, "episode_name": "Ep 52: Kansas Sartin | Never Settle | PAKMULE Cargo Carrier", "slug": "ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "guest_name": "Kansas Sartin", "guest_title": "PAKMULE", "categories": [], "short_description": "Monster beatings and still keeps going", "podcast_length": "44:74"}, {"id": "cfb2c4ed-13a4-4256-8979-2a155515a08f", "episode_id": "d94c730e-2348-4344-99a7-bebeea1a4908", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4859", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (34:41): Yeah. Well, I think as you said, one, I don't think it matters how well your marketing is. If obviously if your product isn't good, then no one's going to buy your stuff. And I think it's cool that you guys have put so much effort into making sure the product is as good as it can be, but while still trying to improve on it with each iteration. I'm curious though, back up a little bit. You've mentioned, obviously your product is for a very niche audience. Not everyone needs a super robust cargo carrier like you guys make. And you mentioned you've worked with some groups, some agencies, some businesses in the past to try to do some digital advertising, but they weren't able to find your audience what wasn't working there. I'm just curious how you guys have now found the right people who Pac Newell is for and if there's anything that our audience can learn from trying to find where your demographic and where your audience is located and how to talk to them.", "token_estimate": 248, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "34:41", "end_timestamp": "34:41", "podcast_index": 52, "episode_name": "Ep 52: Kansas Sartin | Never Settle | PAKMULE Cargo Carrier", "slug": "ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "guest_name": "Kansas Sartin", "guest_title": "PAKMULE", "categories": [], "short_description": "Monster beatings and still keeps going", "podcast_length": "44:74"}, {"id": "e0f0bbd7-e1be-4030-85c2-d8eb6613959c", "episode_id": "d94c730e-2348-4344-99a7-bebeea1a4908", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4859", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Kansas Sartin (35:44): I can't claim to be an expert in digital marketing, and it's still a little bit of a magic potion as far as I'm concerned. And so I think it's really about people paying attention to the data, the customer stream, the touch points, the interactions where you're showing up for them, all of those things that allow it to be successful. I really think you just have to be in it all the time just obsessed about it. The way that I'm obsessed about the welds that are on our pack meal is the way that the right digital marketer has to be obsessed. You can't just plug in some ads and let 'em run for a week and then let 'em fizz out. If they're not doing good, you need to call 'em. And if they're doing really good, you need to start bumping 'em and you need to learn from what went well there and modify it. (36:40): You got to change constantly. And so I'm excited about the ad set that we're just turned on today. We've got great imagery. Some of it's customer provided imagery. We got customer quotes from reviews that have been sent in. So we're just trying to, not from us, but look at what other people are doing and look at what they're saying about it. And then some of it's runtime and some of it's spend. And so the more folks that you can hit and the more that you can learn from those folks, the better the algorithm gets in trying to find those people. And then it's interesting too, I look at regional information a couple of times a week just looking at where pack meals are going. And it's interesting, we might send something to Massachusetts, a town of Massachusetts, and then I'll notice that after that one, maybe a week or so after it was ordered, and so now it's delivered that we'll start seeing another couple that get ordered in that area. (37:54): And I have to suspect that it's that person saying, oh my gosh, this thing is awesome. It's more than what I thought it was going to be just by the images and stuff. And then they're telling their friends and they tell their neighbors and they find somebody there that says, oh, I really need one of those. So then they buy one. And I love to see that. And I think it tells a story in of itself just in the quality of the product and its usefulness and it's lightweight, so it's something that's easy to put on and off. And with the hitching post, you're just hanging on the wall when you're not using it, and it's out of the way. And so just where does it live? Where does it go? How does it go on all those things. If they make sense and it's easy, then people will keep using it. But any other product, if it's too complicated, people won't use it because talk themselves out of it. I don't want to mess with that. I'm just going to cram my stuff in the back and deal with it.", "token_estimate": 694, "speakers": ["Kansas Sartin"], "start_timestamp": "35:44", "end_timestamp": "35:44", "podcast_index": 52, "episode_name": "Ep 52: Kansas Sartin | Never Settle | PAKMULE Cargo Carrier", "slug": "ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "guest_name": "Kansas Sartin", "guest_title": "PAKMULE", "categories": [], "short_description": "Monster beatings and still keeps going", "podcast_length": "44:74"}, {"id": "22e57c95-dcc7-4dda-a2cc-98d7da47a0aa", "episode_id": "d94c730e-2348-4344-99a7-bebeea1a4908", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4859", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Cole Heilborn (38:48): Yeah. Well, I guess that's kind of indicative of your original story of folks stopping you at the Trailhead asking you where you got your original PAC Mule. You can never second guess the power of word of mouth. That is one of the most valuable forms of marketing, and it's cool to see that you can actually track that and see what's happening and that it is working.\n\nKansas Sartin (39:12): Yeah, yeah, yeah, it's been really fun. And honestly, I love talking to customers and I answer the phone sometimes. And the other day I was in the shop and phone rang and somebody from, I think he was in Carolinas or Virginia, somewhere on the east coast and just wanted to chat about the product and say, man, I just love what y'all are doing with the marketing and asking me about certain functions and features and what vehicles it works on and the vehicle he's got. And then says, well, goes, of course, I'm probably providing more information than just somebody else that answers the phone. And he says, are you the owner? I said, yeah, Frankie didn't answer the phone today or was out, had to go take kids to the doctor or something. And so I grabbed the phone. It's like, it's crazy you're answering the phone. That doesn't happen. But also talking to customers is also how we learn pain points. What are they looking for? What are they trying to do? Where are they going? And my absolute favorite thing is when customers send us pictures on their trips and stuff and how they've loaded it and what they're doing and where they're going and smiles and all that stuff. That's my absolute favorite thing to see.\n\nCole Heilborn (40:31): Yeah. Cool, man. Well, I'm stoked for you guys. Last question. What do you hope for the future of PAC Meal your four years in? Where do you want to be in another four years?", "token_estimate": 458, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Kansas Sartin"], "start_timestamp": "38:48", "end_timestamp": "40:31", "podcast_index": 52, "episode_name": "Ep 52: Kansas Sartin | Never Settle | PAKMULE Cargo Carrier", "slug": "ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "guest_name": "Kansas Sartin", "guest_title": "PAKMULE", "categories": [], "short_description": "Monster beatings and still keeps going", "podcast_length": "44:74"}, {"id": "70a220b2-c28d-4090-824f-15d175af34d8", "episode_id": "d94c730e-2348-4344-99a7-bebeea1a4908", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4859", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Kansas Sartin (40:43): Oh man. I have a big vision for Pac Meal. I mean, we want to be the premier cargo hauling brand on the market where people start thinking, how do I do this? Let's check and see what Pac Mill's got that can take care of that. I want to be a problem solver in the market. I want continual to be an innovation leader. But then doing it all responsibly where the majority of the products are built overseas, we're making stuff here in the US and so no greenhouse gases. We're using recycled content. When you open one of our boxes, it's recycled box or content recycled and there's no plastic on the inside. And all of that was predated the plastic movement and metal straws and all that stuff. It was just because I don't want to wrap this huge thing in a bag, and if it's got a little dust on it, it's an outdoor product, just hose it off when you get it, but you now don't have 24 square feet of plastic that you're just going to throw away. (41:42): It was one use. And so just being conscious of what we do and being a company that when I look at leaders in that regard, Patagonia does such a great job in the way that they look at business as a thought leader in environment and sustainability and all those things. And so it's interesting because you get emails from outdoor retailer, it's a trade show for dealers, and it's like how to become more sustainable and how to reduce your impact and all this stuff. And I'm like, man, I'm already in the green and I haven't been to one of these classes. I'm looking good. But I love that and I hope that I to see more manufacturing come back to America and improve jobs. And a lot of that's about safety too. What's the impact on the people that built your product? Are they in a safe work environment? (42:40): Are they able to hug their kids when they come home? At the end of the day, are they able to make a good living and in an environment where they're emotionally taken care of? And there's a lot of stories overseas about all sorts of crazy labor stuff, and I would hate to be a contributor to that by the things that I purchased. And so we're proud about making everything here in Texas and the guys that are welding and the ladies that are cutting metal and recycling the shavings and just doing all that we can to the best of our ability to leave the world a better place for kids and grandkids and all the future generations that will come after us.", "token_estimate": 609, "speakers": ["Kansas Sartin"], "start_timestamp": "40:43", "end_timestamp": "40:43", "podcast_index": 52, "episode_name": "Ep 52: Kansas Sartin | Never Settle | PAKMULE Cargo Carrier", "slug": "ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "guest_name": "Kansas Sartin", "guest_title": "PAKMULE", "categories": [], "short_description": "Monster beatings and still keeps going", "podcast_length": "44:74"}, {"id": "f020b783-85c3-418e-a493-b6a40e4c4270", "episode_id": "d94c730e-2348-4344-99a7-bebeea1a4908", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4859", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Cole Heilborn (43:23): Yeah, well said. Well, Kansas, thank you again for taking the time to chat today. If folks want to follow along with you or the brand, where can they go to connect?\n\nKansas Sartin (43:34): Instagram is a great spot. Pac Meal Gear, P-A-K-M-U-L-E-G-E-A-R, also on Facebook or our website, pme.com.\n\nCole Heilborn (43:43): Cool. Sounds good. Well, I'm sure if folks are in the Texas area, maybe if folks know Rick and Kansas, you guys can all meet together at a trailhead and go hang out and have easy access to your beers. That'd\n\nKansas Sartin (43:59): Be a good time.\n\nCole Heilborn (44:00): We'd love that. Cool, man. Well, thanks again and have a good rest of your day.\n\nKansas Sartin (44:05): Oh, such a pleasure. Happy holidays. I hope your season ends well.\n\nCole Heilborn (44:08): You too. Have a good year. Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learn something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 262, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Kansas Sartin"], "start_timestamp": "43:23", "end_timestamp": "44:08", "podcast_index": 52, "episode_name": "Ep 52: Kansas Sartin | Never Settle | PAKMULE Cargo Carrier", "slug": "ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-52-kansas-sartin-never-settle-pakmule-cargo-carrier-e7254", "guest_name": "Kansas Sartin", "guest_title": "PAKMULE", "categories": [], "short_description": "Monster beatings and still keeps going", "podcast_length": "44:74"}, {"id": "70418e94-5f32-4f3d-9ced-e53828d9ce53", "episode_id": "a2ccd667-5110-411f-9d78-78cf9e0d48d5", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485c", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Zach Vestal (00:13): As a marketer, one of the ways that I sort of try to make decisions plan this and that is to try to step out of the frame and look at myself and my own behavior from an outside perspective.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:33): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hallborn and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam, or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Back Country Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole. Hi, boring. Today I'm sitting down with Zach Vestal, the marketing manager for Niner Bikes and Batch Bicycles. Zach, how are you?\n\nZach Vestal (01:14): Hey, I'm good, thanks. Appreciate being here. Thanks for having me.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:20): No problem. I think we hopefully made it through our technical difficulties. We've spent a few minutes sorting through that, but we're here and I'm excited to chat more.\n\nZach Vestal (01:28): Yeah, me too. Most definitely. It's what are you going to do, distance and technology and fixing things left and right, but it's great. We're rolling.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:38): We're rolling. So where are you calling in from today?\n\nZach Vestal (01:41): So I'm based in Fort Collins, Colorado. I've lived here for just over a year, and I've been working four niner bikes for about two and a half years.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:51): Okay, right on. And yeah, I'd love to dive into your role as marketing manager, but before we get into that, I'm curious to learn a little bit more about Niner and Batch. I definitely, Niner Bikes has definitely made a name for itself in the bike space. I haven't heard so much about batch other than when you, we've been chatting, but it sounds like there's some cool opportunities going on with that brand as well.", "token_estimate": 537, "speakers": ["Zach Vestal", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:13", "end_timestamp": "01:51", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 54: Zach Vestal | Bikes, Storytelling, Growth in Challenge | Niner Bikes", "slug": "ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "guest_name": "Zach Vestal", "guest_title": "Niner Bikes and BATCH Bikes", "categories": [], "short_description": "Pedal Damn It!", "podcast_length": "44:76"}, {"id": "fa60181e-06f2-470d-8ff9-ac7319f1cd4b", "episode_id": "a2ccd667-5110-411f-9d78-78cf9e0d48d5", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485c", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Zach Vestal (02:16): Yeah, yeah, definitely. I'm glad you picked up on that. So, little bit about Niner, little bit about Batch Niner was started in 2005 based in Southern California, and it was based on the premise that 29 inch wheels for mountain bikes are better period across the board, every bike, every situation. And the entire brand was built around this love of 29 er mountain bikes, and we've since expanded to gravel bikes and e-bikes and everything, bikes for dirt, and they've all got the big wheels. The company moved from Southern California to Fort Collins five or 10 years ago, and like I said, I joined the company about two and a half years ago and moved to Fort Collins a year and a half ago. So that's kind of been my ride with Niner Bikes. About three years ago, Niner was acquired by a larger organization based in Ohio, and we were meant to be kind of the tip of the spear for an entire group of brands that's meant to be sold through independent specialty retail bicycle dealers. And that specialty retail group, Niners at the top of it with premium boutiques, high-end bikes for people who really know what they want, and they're probably experienced riders. Our prices range from 3000 to $9,000. (03:49): The company wanted to have a broader offering for bike dealers at every price point, and therefore the batch brand was started. So Batch Bicycles was started about two years ago, and it's really bikes in the $500 range. There's a mountain bike, there's a fitness bike, there's a comfort bike, there's kids bikes, and as with Niner, it's meant to be sold through specialty retailers. In this group, we have an affinity for local retail. We want to support independent dealers, but we also feel they need to have a broad spectrum of bikes to offer their customers. So that's how the batch brand was started. And as you commented earlier, it's been quite a year for that brand.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:43): Oh, I'm sure. Yeah. Does Niners still only run 29 inch wheels?", "token_estimate": 499, "speakers": ["Zach Vestal", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "02:16", "end_timestamp": "04:43", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 54: Zach Vestal | Bikes, Storytelling, Growth in Challenge | Niner Bikes", "slug": "ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "guest_name": "Zach Vestal", "guest_title": "Niner Bikes and BATCH Bikes", "categories": [], "short_description": "Pedal Damn It!", "podcast_length": "44:76"}, {"id": "cf5ac48e-ff87-47bb-85ed-417a66289f32", "episode_id": "a2ccd667-5110-411f-9d78-78cf9e0d48d5", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485c", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Zach Vestal (04:50): There is one bike that was made two years ago or so that had 27.5 inch wheels. We still have a few of those left. The reality is 99% of what we produce and sell is 29 inch wheel bound bikes.\n\nCole Heilborn (05:07): Gotcha, gotcha, man. So yeah, I'm sure Niner Bikes has been super busy this last year. I'm sure batch bikes has been incredibly busy this last year as so many people as we know have just been getting into cycling and breaking into entry level sports kind of everywhere. What's that been like?\n\nZach Vestal (05:30): It's been a wild ride. I mean, it's almost impossible to describe the 12 months that we've kind of been through. I mean, obviously the world has been through just insane disruption and uncertainty and everything else. And that all kind of hit us around this time. Last year is when we started getting news of factory shutdowns and disruptions to the supply chain and everything because it's starting in Southeast Asia where an overwhelming majority of bicycles and bike parts are produced. So that started, we got, I'd say relatively early word about that in part thanks to the batch brand because their manufacturing is in China. (06:21): And then so as the shutdowns hit in March, our business dropped in half because bike shops were closed along with restaurants and businesses, everything. Well, not long after that bike shops were declared essential retail and because soccer and swimming and football, baseball, basketball, bowling lanes, you name it, it was all closed. But the outdoors was still open, right outdoors. So people are running, riding, biking, walking, hiking, and doing all that. And the run the demand on bicycles is like nothing we've ever seen. I mean, absolutely unprecedented. And the batch brand, for example, offering affordable bikes to new cyclists or people that haven't had a bike for a number of years, the company did twice its projection and just insane amount of business.", "token_estimate": 490, "speakers": ["Zach Vestal", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "04:50", "end_timestamp": "05:30", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 54: Zach Vestal | Bikes, Storytelling, Growth in Challenge | Niner Bikes", "slug": "ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "guest_name": "Zach Vestal", "guest_title": "Niner Bikes and BATCH Bikes", "categories": [], "short_description": "Pedal Damn It!", "podcast_length": "44:76"}, {"id": "aa1b35ff-ad81-4852-a21d-c32e9b13a2f5", "episode_id": "a2ccd667-5110-411f-9d78-78cf9e0d48d5", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485c", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (07:33): And you look at the batch website now, and I'm sure it's two things, but most bikes are sold out and one, I'm sure those are just lingering production challenges. Two, it's probably just the increase in demand that you guys have had.\n\nZach Vestal (07:47): Yes, exactly. So nobody saw this coming, to be honest, in February when we started getting worried about the global economy forecasts were actually reduced and production was slowed down, and this was deliberate because nobody saw the whiplash back in the other direction. So the ripple effect from raw materials to small parts to component pieces, to labor shortages, shipping bottlenecks, it's just all compounding to cause us to run out of a number of popular models with long lead times to get those replaced. So we've got bike orders from October that have yet to be delivered, and we're not the only company with that. So it's not just batch, it's not just niner, it's everybody, it's bananas. But like you said, it's awesome to see the sport having this moment. It's great.\n\nCole Heilborn (08:54): Oh my gosh, just going up on the local trails here in Bellingham, there's so many more people out on an average day, the weather isn't great, it's like a Wednesday afternoon and people are just, there's just so many more people running and biking. It's so cool. So through all this, I guess, what's been the biggest challenges specific to your job as the marketing manager? What do you oversee? What do you work on and what have you been working through?", "token_estimate": 385, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Zach Vestal"], "start_timestamp": "07:33", "end_timestamp": "08:54", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 54: Zach Vestal | Bikes, Storytelling, Growth in Challenge | Niner Bikes", "slug": "ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "guest_name": "Zach Vestal", "guest_title": "Niner Bikes and BATCH Bikes", "categories": [], "short_description": "Pedal Damn It!", "podcast_length": "44:76"}, {"id": "c2c9bc00-5fd6-40dd-9639-d2d00bec3489", "episode_id": "a2ccd667-5110-411f-9d78-78cf9e0d48d5", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485c", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Zach Vestal (09:25): Yeah, yeah, a couple good questions in there. So I guess we should start with what I work on because that leads to the others. So as marketing manager for Niner Bikes and now recently Batch Bicycles as well, my number one job is to make sure that we're representing and communicating our products to the riding public and to our dealers. And a lot of that starts with some of the most fundamentally basic things that we don't even think about when we're, who knows, shopping on Amazon for a book or something. So photography, we have to have pictures of our bikes on our websites, catalog pages. We need pictures obviously for social media and whatever else. So if we've got a, for example, new frame color, we like to put parts on that, take pictures of the complete bike, put those on our website. So that's just one aspect of my job, making sure that we're able to display and communicate our stuff in really fundamental ways. (10:34): And one part of my job that's been pretty hard lately is we can't even get parts to put on the frames to take pictures. We've got an e-bike, it's called the RIP E nine, and it's one of our first e-bikes ever, and it's turned out to be super popular, which is great. We had a specification change effective late November, just different tires, different fork, whatever. I haven't been able to take a picture of that yet for the website. So the website just has a disclaimer, says, Hey, photo is not necessarily representative of actual bicycle spec. So that's just one little example of the challenges that we're facing right now. But at the end of the day, it's all good. It's fun. My job is to tell the stories, get people excited, bring the brand to life, and we do that through every lever that you can kind of pull from a marketing standpoint. So it's obviously photography content such as video and text, it's email communicating by email via social media, little bit of advertising, not much right now, but that's part of the mix. Sponsored athletes and ambassadors, influencers, that kind of thing. I'm basically responsible, me and a team of two other dudes are responsible for the entire presentation of niner bikes to the world.", "token_estimate": 550, "speakers": ["Zach Vestal"], "start_timestamp": "09:25", "end_timestamp": "09:25", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 54: Zach Vestal | Bikes, Storytelling, Growth in Challenge | Niner Bikes", "slug": "ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "guest_name": "Zach Vestal", "guest_title": "Niner Bikes and BATCH Bikes", "categories": [], "short_description": "Pedal Damn It!", "podcast_length": "44:76"}, {"id": "5668223e-533c-42dc-8b34-2ea277f90ef0", "episode_id": "a2ccd667-5110-411f-9d78-78cf9e0d48d5", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485c", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole Heilborn (12:12): Gotcha, man. So of those different avenues that you mentioned, I'm curious what has been working really well in the last year. Are there certain channels that have grown? Are there certain channels that have decreased and how have you guys responded to that?\n\nZach Vestal (12:30): Good question. So it is interesting in marketing to keep your finger on the pulse of where people are spending their time, where are they spending their attention, whether it's ears or eyeballs or in some cases events like physical presence. Where do you find people, where do you access people? It has changed significantly. Events obviously are out the window, so we're no longer doing any forms of experiential marketing to speak of, and that's normally one of your best opportunities to sort of on a one-on-one basis or relatively intimate basis, get people really familiar with your products, your people, your brand. So that's out the window. We've seen a huge surge in interest on YouTube, obviously with more time at home, less time in the office, little bit less ability to go out and do stuff, less travel, that kind of thing. YouTube traffic has really surged, so that's been where we've tried to dedicate some effort with some ambassadors who ride our bikes, make their own content, we let them do their thing, but they're on niner bikes and we get good feedback from that. And I'm sure you're aware in the advertising space, podcasts are a pretty strong sort of outlet for advertising because generally people's attention is a little more focused than it is looking at a website, for example, where we've learned to visually filter boxes and banners.", "token_estimate": 417, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Zach Vestal"], "start_timestamp": "12:12", "end_timestamp": "12:30", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 54: Zach Vestal | Bikes, Storytelling, Growth in Challenge | Niner Bikes", "slug": "ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "guest_name": "Zach Vestal", "guest_title": "Niner Bikes and BATCH Bikes", "categories": [], "short_description": "Pedal Damn It!", "podcast_length": "44:76"}, {"id": "3c59d705-a22e-4a0c-ba69-0c43f3822349", "episode_id": "a2ccd667-5110-411f-9d78-78cf9e0d48d5", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485c", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (14:22): Right. Well, yeah, I mean, this podcast is one of many thousands or hundreds of thousands that were started during COVID. So yeah, it's cool to see that channel kind of take off coming back to YouTube with the ambassadors. Yeah. So you mentioned that you're seeing a bunch of traffic and a bunch of channel growth and engagement on YouTube. How are you working with your ambassadors though to help them? Are you encouraging to make more content? Are you publishing and creating stuff for your own channel? Are you just working with the ambassadors and then taking what they make and put it on your own channel? What does that look like?\n\nZach Vestal (15:05): Yeah, good question. It kind of varies. It varies by what our goals are, what do we want to accomplish. Over the course of the last year, we affiliated with Sid and Mackey who have about 60, 65,000 subscribers, and we also affiliated with Kyle Warner, who started the year with not so many subscribers, and he's ended the year with over a hundred thousand. He's seen huge channel growth, and in part it's because he got his girlfriend April on the screen and he's been doing series of instructional videos. So honestly, for me and for Niner bikes, it starts with just making sure they have all the niner bikes that they need to do what they need to do, and I really try to take a hands-off role. I just want them to be themselves, do their thing, and they have great personality and they're authentic, sincere, they really represent the niner values. And honestly, that's all I need. That's all I'm going for. That being said, on one or two occasions we've had new bikes that we've wanted to get the word out about, and so I'll specifically ask for, Hey, can we figure something out to do a first look, a first ride, check this one out. We've done that on a limited basis, but you don't want to do that too much because erode their channel authenticity.\n\nCole Heilborn (16:41): Are you finding that people are, is there a specific type of content that people are consuming more of? Is it like adventure lifestyle stuff? Is it bike review because people are trying to purchase bikes? What are they looking at more?", "token_estimate": 549, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Zach Vestal"], "start_timestamp": "14:22", "end_timestamp": "16:41", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 54: Zach Vestal | Bikes, Storytelling, Growth in Challenge | Niner Bikes", "slug": "ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "guest_name": "Zach Vestal", "guest_title": "Niner Bikes and BATCH Bikes", "categories": [], "short_description": "Pedal Damn It!", "podcast_length": "44:76"}, {"id": "08c7909f-f97d-460b-ad20-f049c7745bd3", "episode_id": "a2ccd667-5110-411f-9d78-78cf9e0d48d5", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485c", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Zach Vestal (16:57): We're seeing a huge growth in the instructional type of content. People want to learn how to do the stuff because who knows, maybe you were going to the gym three days a week and you've got that dialed, you had a trainer and then you took off and started your own exercise program. You're good to go. Well, Jim closed, all right, dusting off my 10-year-old mountain bike, getting it fixed, maybe buying a new mountain bike or whatever. But everything's different. It's like, oh, my word trails are different, the bike is different. I'm watching people do this and do that, and how do I do that? So I think that speaks a lot to the success of Kyle and April. Sid and Mackey have taken a bit of a different approach, and it's a lot about little bit different places, but they haven't been able to travel a lot, so they've sort of adapted and they've done some bike repair videos like how to fix the thing. (18:03): The bike can be a complicated machine to somebody who hasn't been into it for a long time. So I'd say instructional videos are huge. And then Dylan Johnson is also a sponsored athlete of ours, and he's got a YouTube channel and he's a coach, so he's been kind of approaching it more from the athletic performance side of things, and there seems to be a decent appetite for that. Again, I think because people are feeling isolated and it's fun to get tips and support and learn from people who clearly have some experience. That's what I'm seeing, I'd say the most of, at least amongst our group.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:48): Yeah. Do you feel like that's specific to the bike industry or is that kind of a generalization you can make about the whole outdoor space right now?", "token_estimate": 426, "speakers": ["Zach Vestal", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "16:57", "end_timestamp": "18:48", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 54: Zach Vestal | Bikes, Storytelling, Growth in Challenge | Niner Bikes", "slug": "ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "guest_name": "Zach Vestal", "guest_title": "Niner Bikes and BATCH Bikes", "categories": [], "short_description": "Pedal Damn It!", "podcast_length": "44:76"}, {"id": "ab364a1f-a200-4bde-ba10-931f86b84807", "episode_id": "a2ccd667-5110-411f-9d78-78cf9e0d48d5", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485c", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Zach Vestal (18:56): Gosh, that's a really good question, and I should be probably more, my spidey senses should probably have a better sense of what else is out there in the interwebs. The truth is, I can barely keep track of our own crew, but one of the ways, as a marketer, one of the ways that I sort of try to make decisions plan this and that is to try to step out of the frame and look at myself and my own behavior from an outside perspective. And I do that when I'm, let's say if I have a problem I need to solve or something, either a new activity that I'm into or something I need to fix, what are my behaviors as I go about trying to do this or do that? And from that perspective at least I would say that the instructional, or I don't know if educational type of content is just anecdotally, I would say educational types of content are probably outperforming, whether it's education about a product or how to do a thing. That's the kind of stuff I find myself gravitating to, and I have a strong suspicion that that's the kind of stuff that's performing well broadly.\n\nCole Heilborn (20:20): Yeah, I could see it falling into two camps. There's the educational, I want to learn how to fix my bike, or I want to learn how to change the oil in my car. I got a little more time on my hands. I can also see, I'm thinking a lot of the short films that REI and Patagonia puts out, I could see those seeing a massive surge in viewership just because people are trying to escape vicariously live whatever. They're just exploring and they can't explore everywhere they want to right now. Maybe they can explore YouTube and see what other people are up to.", "token_estimate": 415, "speakers": ["Zach Vestal", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "18:56", "end_timestamp": "20:20", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 54: Zach Vestal | Bikes, Storytelling, Growth in Challenge | Niner Bikes", "slug": "ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "guest_name": "Zach Vestal", "guest_title": "Niner Bikes and BATCH Bikes", "categories": [], "short_description": "Pedal Damn It!", "podcast_length": "44:76"}, {"id": "a32aee65-4b61-45ad-b0f3-5bdf50e46433", "episode_id": "a2ccd667-5110-411f-9d78-78cf9e0d48d5", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485c", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Zach Vestal (20:59): Yeah. Yeah, a hundred percent. That's a really good point. A hundred percent the sort of vicarious tourism, being able to go and see a new trail, see a new country, this and that. I can't physically do it, but I can do it through my computer screen. So yeah, a hundred percent I can see that. I would say that those types of films haven't done as well for us. There's probably other factors that fall into play there, but I think that's a good question. I don't know. We have to be careful budget wise, and so our house video isn't as highly polished. It's not as awesome. We don't go to the far, far-flung places, so we do our best on a shoestring. And when you're up against Patagonia in that kind of genre of, we'll call it a brand film, we're just not going to win that competition for people's time and attention. (22:09): And that's fine. I totally recognize that. So I'd say that's part of it. I also think that the high production value brand videos from bigger companies probably do well. Smaller companies, like I said, don't have as much resource to do those really, really well. And I think there's a benefit to the third party aspect of doing that type of thing for a smaller brand through an athlete, through an ambassador, through a content creator, rather than trying to publish it as brand content. Patagonia has a great name, so it's easy to click on that video and say, yeah, I want to see what Patagonia has to show me. N we're pretty small. Do we really have enough brand recognition to get somebody to say, yeah, I'm going to spend five minutes watching what these guys are up to. I don't know if we have that kind of pull. Whereas if it's coming from one of our athletes who's kind of independently established a degree of credibility or fan base, I think it's easier for somebody to commit their attention to that.", "token_estimate": 465, "speakers": ["Zach Vestal"], "start_timestamp": "20:59", "end_timestamp": "20:59", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 54: Zach Vestal | Bikes, Storytelling, Growth in Challenge | Niner Bikes", "slug": "ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "guest_name": "Zach Vestal", "guest_title": "Niner Bikes and BATCH Bikes", "categories": [], "short_description": "Pedal Damn It!", "podcast_length": "44:76"}, {"id": "b35a0e5a-58ec-450a-be3b-427664017e89", "episode_id": "a2ccd667-5110-411f-9d78-78cf9e0d48d5", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485c", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Cole Heilborn (23:31): Yeah, I do think it's talking about production quality and production quality associated with value and value associated with engagement. On YouTube. Obviously we're kind of comparing apples and oranges. Patagonia obviously is who they are, and they bring a lot to the table when they produce one of those films. But I think what's interesting and what I see in the space, despite just the production quality challenges that so many brands have just due to budget and time and resources and all that stuff, I think what's kind of at the heart of all those films that we love is just the great storytelling. And I think that's one area that I definitely see smaller brands maybe not paying as much attention to. And sure, Patagonia's got the great production quality, but they also have an amazing story that they're telling, and people could care less about the quality as long as the story's there. But I think sometimes that story development piece is sometimes forgotten or not as much work goes into it.\n\nZach Vestal (24:39): That's a really good comment. I think that's a really good comment, and it's pretty accurate. In the case of Niner, I'm going to be honest, I'm not afraid to look in the mirror for my brands, my own work, whatever it is. And I will be a hundred percent candid with you and say that story development is one of the bigger challenges as a small organization trying to keep all the plates spinning and all the balls in the air. I have literally had, I've sent an athlete and a filmer or videographer out on assignment, and I've honestly literally kind of shooed them out the door and just said, yeah, do whatever. Just make it look sweet. And that's about all the guidance I've been able to provide, and I'm sure they just are scratching their heads and Come on, we need more to work with. And they usually turn up something pretty good. But yeah, it really helps if you have a good story as the backbone, and you're totally right.", "token_estimate": 496, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Zach Vestal"], "start_timestamp": "23:31", "end_timestamp": "24:39", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 54: Zach Vestal | Bikes, Storytelling, Growth in Challenge | Niner Bikes", "slug": "ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "guest_name": "Zach Vestal", "guest_title": "Niner Bikes and BATCH Bikes", "categories": [], "short_description": "Pedal Damn It!", "podcast_length": "44:76"}, {"id": "89ae9ced-c98f-455b-9206-a84aaef116ea", "episode_id": "a2ccd667-5110-411f-9d78-78cf9e0d48d5", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485c", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Cole Heilborn (25:42): Well, and you guys aren't totally alone. I'm not trying to single you guys out, but there's just so many companies that to do a story well, it takes a ton of work. Do you feel like there's a way to do that where you guys can still tell a good story, but I don't know, some workarounds or some ways you can make it easier for yourself? I don't know. I guess for other folks that are listening too, if there's anything that you've learned that you could pass on to other people. I don't know. Any advice.\n\nZach Vestal (26:14): Oh gosh, I would love it. I don't know if you're able to take comments or some such, but I would love to entertain a forum and hear people's ideas on how to do a little bit better with storytelling. I would say a, if I could do it over again with a certain few little projects we've done, I would definitely just take the half an hour, quiet my mind, maybe get somebody in the room, bounce some ideas around. Honestly, (26:53): It's for me, the company I work with, it's just a matter of bandwidth and discipline. And I think if you just simply take a moment and figure it out, just to cite the counter example, the times when we've done the work, the times when we had the creative input, it was a couple years ago, we honestly had more staff. We had one person in particular who was particularly creative, and she came up with some fun ideas, and those videos went on to actually perform pretty well. One of 'em was like a Napoleon Dynamite knockoff, and it was just, oh gosh, a short, yeah, I don't know if you saw that, but it was just kind of a late\n\nCole Heilborn (27:40): No, but that's my favorite movie.", "token_estimate": 415, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Zach Vestal"], "start_timestamp": "25:42", "end_timestamp": "27:40", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 54: Zach Vestal | Bikes, Storytelling, Growth in Challenge | Niner Bikes", "slug": "ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "guest_name": "Zach Vestal", "guest_title": "Niner Bikes and BATCH Bikes", "categories": [], "short_description": "Pedal Damn It!", "podcast_length": "44:76"}, {"id": "ad3dd005-7d07-40f4-8183-26f7b27078d0", "episode_id": "a2ccd667-5110-411f-9d78-78cf9e0d48d5", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485c", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Zach Vestal (27:43): So there you go. Yeah, who doesn't love Napoleon Dynamite? And she came up with this cool little sequence and found the right videographer to give it a nice touch. And I don't even think it was 60 seconds, but it actually did really well, made people smile, was perfect for social, and that's what you want. There was another one, the through line, the narrative in air quotes, if you will, was basically a rider on a bike delivering newspapers, and it just kind of casts the activity of cycling with a fun slant that we haven't even, it's like a historic relic, isn't it? Like delivering newspapers\n\nCole Heilborn (28:28): For a\n\nZach Vestal (28:29): Bike. I mean, come on. But to put that person on a carbon fiber, full suspension, gravel bike, ripping through the neighborhoods, you're like, okay, that's freaking brilliant. And it doesn't have to be anything more complicated than that. It just has to give you a bit of a backbone, and then you can spin a story up from that. Whereas the ones where it's like gravel rider on gravel roads in the desert of Arizona, I love that stuff. It's beautiful. But to your point, there's no real hook there.\n\nCole Heilborn (29:05): Yeah. Well, it's been done before. When it first came out, it was cool. It was like, oh, that's awesome. But now everyone's like, we've seen it. We're tired of it. We want something new. And that's got to be tough for the folks in your position where you're like, you have hundreds of other plates that are spinning and there's this one that's always changing on you and having to constantly adapt and evolve with that. Yeah, I mean, that's got to be tough, especially when you're dealing with production challenges and you can't even get a product in the house, no product.", "token_estimate": 443, "speakers": ["Zach Vestal", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "27:43", "end_timestamp": "29:05", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 54: Zach Vestal | Bikes, Storytelling, Growth in Challenge | Niner Bikes", "slug": "ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "guest_name": "Zach Vestal", "guest_title": "Niner Bikes and BATCH Bikes", "categories": [], "short_description": "Pedal Damn It!", "podcast_length": "44:76"}, {"id": "ee032280-36fa-45f4-b185-0e37521a922f", "episode_id": "a2ccd667-5110-411f-9d78-78cf9e0d48d5", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485c", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Zach Vestal (29:40): Yeah, I hate to make excuses or whatever it is. And it's like, yeah, everybody's got problems. And I consider it sort of one of the personal challenges that I need to rise to is just put a little more attention to it, tend to, everybody's got their strengths and weaknesses. I really like the written word. I like writing. That's where my best creativity comes out. And so it's too easy for me to just sort of turn over the video work and kind of let people run with it, whereas just a little bit of effort upfront will yield a lot better, a lot work. But as I say, I'm really glad, we're really lucky to have athletes and ambassadors that I have total confidence in to do what they need to do. And just by association with niner bikes, we've already felt a bit of a lift and that's good.\n\nCole Heilborn (30:38): Yeah, that's awesome. So switching gears a little bit, I'm curious, as a marketing manager, you're overseeing two different brands, batch and Niner, both of which are very different, very high end, more of the entry world. Does that present challenges for you as you're kind of overseeing both of those, just that they're so different?", "token_estimate": 293, "speakers": ["Zach Vestal", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "29:40", "end_timestamp": "30:38", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 54: Zach Vestal | Bikes, Storytelling, Growth in Challenge | Niner Bikes", "slug": "ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "guest_name": "Zach Vestal", "guest_title": "Niner Bikes and BATCH Bikes", "categories": [], "short_description": "Pedal Damn It!", "podcast_length": "44:76"}, {"id": "71d608cc-bee0-4739-a5db-3e45939bba03", "episode_id": "a2ccd667-5110-411f-9d78-78cf9e0d48d5", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485c", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Zach Vestal (31:01): Yeah. Yeah. It's challenging for sure, no doubt. I mean, there are certain marketing fundamentals which are just consistent across no matter what you're doing, one of which I mentioned early on, just the basics, copywriting, photography, part numbers and prices. Just making sure everybody has the information they need to do what they need to do, whether it's on the sales team or retailers or the riding public. So the basic sort of, I'll call it ground game or blocking and tackling, it's fairly consistent across. But yes, I've had to think differently about communication for batch bicycles. I've had to think differently about the riding public that is considering this purchase and what does their sort of purchase funnel or customer journey, whatever buzzword you want to assign to it, what does that look like compared to niner bikes? And I've been in sort of the higher end with several brands previously. And so niner bikes marketing activity comes fairly naturally, or at least I feel pretty good, pretty fluent, and I've definitely had to rethink a few things for batch bicycles.\n\nCole Heilborn (32:30): Yeah. Can you give us some examples? What areas are you growing in?", "token_estimate": 302, "speakers": ["Zach Vestal", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "31:01", "end_timestamp": "32:30", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 54: Zach Vestal | Bikes, Storytelling, Growth in Challenge | Niner Bikes", "slug": "ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "guest_name": "Zach Vestal", "guest_title": "Niner Bikes and BATCH Bikes", "categories": [], "short_description": "Pedal Damn It!", "podcast_length": "44:76"}, {"id": "3a7a0e38-fc74-4909-8b6f-f57b75286d47", "episode_id": "a2ccd667-5110-411f-9d78-78cf9e0d48d5", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485c", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Zach Vestal (32:34): Sure. Well, we were just talking about video for niner bikes. We want to show obviously a performance aspect of our bikes, and then also portray riders going to amazing places and looking the part of experienced riders and doing the things. And when you associate your product and your brand name with that, people have a sense of inspiration or aspiration. Yeah, that gravel bike could take me to gravel roads in Mongolia or whatever. It's batch bicycles. That's a very different sort of audience. And I guess my gut feeling, I don't have data to back it up, but I don't feel like at that level there's such a high degree of research on the product or need for brand affinity or whatever. I think there's a lot more for that customer that's happening in the retail store. I sort of perceive a batch bikes customer probably hasn't had a bike for a number of years if ever, and doesn't feel comfortable, doesn't feel comfortable ordering online because it's a bicycle. (33:55): It's like, ah, I don't know. So they want to go to a local shop, going to the local shop, kind of intimidating, lots of different bikes, lots of different brands. It's almost a different language being spoken. So for batch, it's going to be a lot about relatively approachable copywriting, relatively inviting photography that makes it look like, yeah, I could do that. That's within my range. And then also sort of giving the folks in the bike shops the tools to communicate effectively. Yeah, it's a good bike. It's not intimidating. It's going to do what you need it to do. It's a great price. Maybe calling the brand out at the retail level with some store displays and such to kind of draw the eye to, again, this photography and light copywriting that's like, oh yeah, that's a bike. I can get this other one. What is all that with the shocks and the carbon and the $5,000 price tag? I don't want that. But yeah, this one looks pretty like I could get there. So that's sort how I'm trying to approach batch. Honestly, I've only been at it with this brand for since October, so it's relatively new, but as we get spun up, that's the approach that I'm taking.", "token_estimate": 541, "speakers": ["Zach Vestal"], "start_timestamp": "32:34", "end_timestamp": "32:34", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 54: Zach Vestal | Bikes, Storytelling, Growth in Challenge | Niner Bikes", "slug": "ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "guest_name": "Zach Vestal", "guest_title": "Niner Bikes and BATCH Bikes", "categories": [], "short_description": "Pedal Damn It!", "podcast_length": "44:76"}, {"id": "1890733d-bfe1-4f15-9974-c2d3d6acf5e3", "episode_id": "a2ccd667-5110-411f-9d78-78cf9e0d48d5", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485c", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Cole Heilborn (35:24): Yeah, I love that. So yeah, what are you working on right now? Are you getting those in-store displays and copy points all written? Is that kind of what the day-to-day looks like currently?\n\nZach Vestal (35:37): Yeah, so currently the day-to-day is right now trying to get photography done. We've got a color change happening over the next six or eight weeks, I guess. And again, crazy, it's fluid because maybe the container missed the boat and it's going to be on the next boat, which is going to be two months from now. Or maybe there's not enough empty containers to send back to come back loaded with bikes. So our deliveries are fairly fluid. But anyways, six to eight weeks-ish color change, trying to get samples, trying to get photography, trying to get some catalog pages done, trying to get some charts updated so that retailers have access to that info. Studio photography, lifestyle photography. Those are my hot buttons. And honestly, right now I'm just trying to get some hats and t-shirts out to sales staff, retailers. Just being honest, when you've been waiting two months for a bike, a t-shirt goes a long way to sort of help somebody feel a little bit better about just waiting another two weeks or whatever it's going to take.\n\nCole Heilborn (36:54): Yeah. Yeah, man, that's cool. That's got to be just that position you're in. I'm assuming as you kind of pull some of your focus away from Niner and start to look towards batch and start to, excuse me, work through some of those specific challenges, you're probably then going to start to look back at nine and see other opportunities or ways to tweak things and revise it. So I feel like you're probably in a pretty good position just to always be making sure you're growing and learning.", "token_estimate": 446, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Zach Vestal"], "start_timestamp": "35:24", "end_timestamp": "36:54", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 54: Zach Vestal | Bikes, Storytelling, Growth in Challenge | Niner Bikes", "slug": "ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "guest_name": "Zach Vestal", "guest_title": "Niner Bikes and BATCH Bikes", "categories": [], "short_description": "Pedal Damn It!", "podcast_length": "44:76"}, {"id": "9c43ff8d-781f-40d3-abee-b43536077e36", "episode_id": "a2ccd667-5110-411f-9d78-78cf9e0d48d5", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485c", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Zach Vestal (37:23): A hundred percent. It's been a really good growth opportunity for me personally as a marketer. It's caused me to stretch boundaries and do something different. Personally, I come at it from the hardcore cycling. I was a competitive cyclist for whatever and worked for a competitive team for a while, and it's always been the super premium where it is truly a different language. And then to, it took me a solid three or four weeks to even wrap my head around batch bicycles. I didn't even know where to start. And it's been a really good exercise from a personal growth standpoint. And I don't know if you can tell, but it honestly kind of gets me excited too, because I'm so optimistic about the number of new riders coming to bicycles. If for me personally, if I zoom way, way out, I personally believe that bicycles have high potential to save the world. And so I look at batch as like, yeah, man, let's save the freaking world I'm in. Let's do it.\n\nCole Heilborn (38:39): Yeah. Oh man, that's so good. And that's even something you could casually start to incorporate into the language you're using today with Batch. It's very easy to pair that sort of down to earth language, I feel like with that kind of cruiser simplistic mentality.", "token_estimate": 316, "speakers": ["Zach Vestal", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "37:23", "end_timestamp": "38:39", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 54: Zach Vestal | Bikes, Storytelling, Growth in Challenge | Niner Bikes", "slug": "ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "guest_name": "Zach Vestal", "guest_title": "Niner Bikes and BATCH Bikes", "categories": [], "short_description": "Pedal Damn It!", "podcast_length": "44:76"}, {"id": "3023e377-7ce4-4dcf-9e6d-4231ca6ef342", "episode_id": "a2ccd667-5110-411f-9d78-78cf9e0d48d5", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485c", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Zach Vestal (39:02): A hundred percent. Yeah, exactly. And I should give credit real quick to some of the other brands in cycling that have already gone down this path as far as, and the advocacy organizations that have banging on this drum for years, that bicycles are an efficient mode of transportation. They're good for personal health, they're good for psychological health, good for communities. Like the Bicycle has the potential for good in so many ways, and batches just following on the footsteps of the work and sort of language that's already been used by a number of other brands. But you're absolutely right. This is the kind of inspirational, positive messaging that I want to incorporate as we move forward with it. I'm just hoping, obviously those of us that are all hardcore and stuff, we sort of hope that the new riders will work their way up to a Niner bike or whatever and get serious about riding is a real serious thing. But honestly, I don't even care. I do not care if you ride your bike to school once a week. That's awesome. That's awesome. Just do that just once a week. Ride your bike to school or to work or to the store or to the bar. It's good for you. It's good for the planet. It'll make you happy.\n\nCole Heilborn (40:31): It all adds up.\n\nZach Vestal (40:32): It adds up. Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (40:35): Yeah, man. Well, I think you said it in a beautiful way and it's probably a good place to start to wrap it up. I love that positive note to end on. Me too. Zach, if people want to find you, if they want to find Niner or Batch, where can people go to follow along?", "token_estimate": 401, "speakers": ["Zach Vestal", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "39:02", "end_timestamp": "40:35", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 54: Zach Vestal | Bikes, Storytelling, Growth in Challenge | Niner Bikes", "slug": "ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "guest_name": "Zach Vestal", "guest_title": "Niner Bikes and BATCH Bikes", "categories": [], "short_description": "Pedal Damn It!", "podcast_length": "44:76"}, {"id": "3c01af05-7454-44ac-9943-dff85fd6636f", "episode_id": "a2ccd667-5110-411f-9d78-78cf9e0d48d5", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485c", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Zach Vestal (40:58): The first point of contact is going to be the website for each brand, batch bicycles.com, and then niner bikes.com. There are email addresses on those websites actually for both brands. We take a pretty fair amount of pride in the fact that we publish an email address, publish a phone number, you get a person. We are very busy. Might take us a while to get back to you, but that's the first point of contact. You can find me on LinkedIn. I don't personally maintain a social media channel for various reasons, which I don't need to go into. But you can also find Niner and Batch on social media. So we're out there.\n\nCole Heilborn (41:39): Awesome. And if people are curious to collaborate with you on how to tell stories in a better, more efficient, maybe slightly cheaper way, where can they go to brainstorm with you?\n\nZach Vestal (41:55): Do you want me to give a personal email address or\n\nCole Heilborn (41:59): Whatever you're comfortable with?\n\nZach Vestal (42:00): I would say send me a message on LinkedIn. I'm easy to find there. And that goes straight to my inbox. And then I'll give you my email address through there and we'll bounce around some ideas about video because love telling good stories and I'm also interested in ideas.\n\nCole Heilborn (42:20): Right on. Cool. Well, Zach, thanks again for taking the time to chat today, and I hope you have a good rest of your day and maybe get out and do a quick lap on the bike.\n\nZach Vestal (42:29): Yeah, man. You bet. Thank you so much for having me. It's super fun. And to everybody out there, go ride your bike. It's awesome. It'll make you happy.\n\nCole Heilborn (42:38): Alright, take care. Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learn something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 473, "speakers": ["Zach Vestal", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "40:58", "end_timestamp": "42:38", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 54: Zach Vestal | Bikes, Storytelling, Growth in Challenge | Niner Bikes", "slug": "ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-54-zach-vestal-bikes-storytelling-growth-in-challenge-niner-bikes", "guest_name": "Zach Vestal", "guest_title": "Niner Bikes and BATCH Bikes", "categories": [], "short_description": "Pedal Damn It!", "podcast_length": "44:76"}, {"id": "50a836cb-0cb9-4136-802d-004023273ced", "episode_id": "ee39605a-3ae6-4fee-b613-c613abdb9110", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485d", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Scott Holley (00:02): Every paddler has a story. Every paddler is a story, the story of that particular outing, the story of how they got into the sport, the story of what that sport means to them. And there really is no shortcut to telling stories when you're talking about your paddling community other than investing time and effort into listening, understanding, curating, and then from a very consent driven perspective, telling those stories to the general public.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:33): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing podcast. My name is Cole Hallborn and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Back Country Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Port Side Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Holborn. Today I'm sitting down with Scott Hawley, the president and co-owner of Eddie Lion Kayak. Scott, how are you?\n\nScott Holley (01:14): I'm doing great. How are you doing Cole? Good to be with you today.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:18): I'm doing good. Yeah, thank you. I'm excited to be chatting today. It's funny, we met just a few weeks ago and I had no idea, but Eddie Line is like 30 minutes south of Bellingham.\n\nScott Holley (01:32): We're just right off of I five in between Seattle and Vancouver in the most gorgeous place in the country, in my opinion.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:40): It's not bad. It's quite gorgeous. So I'm curious to learn a little bit more about you, about your role about Edline, but first if you could just kind of give us a background on the company and what you guys do.", "token_estimate": 481, "speakers": ["Scott Holley", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:02", "end_timestamp": "01:40", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 55: Scott Holley | On Water Marketing | Eddyline Kayak", "slug": "ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "guest_name": "Scott Holley", "guest_title": "President, Eddyline Kayaks", "categories": [], "short_description": "User Group Guerrilla Style Marketing", "podcast_length": "44:77"}, {"id": "6c6cc972-99dc-4ad8-85aa-b5fe8301e19c", "episode_id": "ee39605a-3ae6-4fee-b613-c613abdb9110", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485d", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Scott Holley (01:55): Yeah, absolutely. So Eline is a manufacturer of kayaks in the recreational and touring segments for Flatwater kayaks specifically. And this is our 50th anniversary. So we were founded in 1971 by Tom de Dare. The history of the company really goes back to the early days of sea kayaking in North America, A lot of intersections with a lot of the other great brands out there. Werner Paddles, the patriarch of Werner Paddles. Werner Fur was the original designer of the Eddie Line. The first Eddie Line kayak really taught Tom Dare how to make kayaks. And of course, back in those days it was all hand layup of fiberglass and composite material. And we've had quite a few innovations since then, but we're still true to our roots. We're here in the northwest in some of the best flat flatwater kayaking destinations in the country. And yeah, we're a family owned company, family and management owned company, privately held, and we love what we do here.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:02): Well said. And I'd be curious to look, actually, you might know this, how do the San Juans rank in terms of kayaking access for the country?\n\nScott Holley (03:13): Boy, that's a great question. The short answer is, I don't know. The slightly longer answer is Washington State actually doesn't have a tourism commission, which is kind of a glaring hole because I've tried to get that information as well, and you just can't do it. So hopefully the state might come up with funding to start getting some better stats on that. And if not, maybe the industry can get together. I know that it's in the tens of thousands of visits each year, and it's listed as the number one reason why people travel to the San Juans is kayaking.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:52): Interesting. The whole lack of tourism department at the state level is interesting. I read some reports and our recreation economy is actually bigger than the, I think it's the tech economy here in Washington, yet we don't have any sort of tourism program.", "token_estimate": 501, "speakers": ["Scott Holley", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:55", "end_timestamp": "03:52", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 55: Scott Holley | On Water Marketing | Eddyline Kayak", "slug": "ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "guest_name": "Scott Holley", "guest_title": "President, Eddyline Kayaks", "categories": [], "short_description": "User Group Guerrilla Style Marketing", "podcast_length": "44:77"}, {"id": "0143b795-80f0-4b9f-8174-d98ca4d62400", "episode_id": "ee39605a-3ae6-4fee-b613-c613abdb9110", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485d", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Scott Holley (04:11): It's the same size as aviation and we have. So it's huge. It's massive, and yeah, it's a great opportunity I think, going into the future to hopefully get something together that can really make sure that we have a plan and strategy for outdoor recreation in the state. But yeah, it'ss a great place to recreate in Washington and Pacific Northwest.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:35): So you guys do all your manufacturing down in Burlington, right? You manufacture a hundred percent here?\n\nScott Holley (04:41): We do. So a little bit more of that background, but we actually have some really interesting intersections with the aviation industry going back into the early nineties when Eline was doing a hundred percent composite fiberglass production of their touring and sea kayaks. The company was located next to a bunch of aviation companies, and Eline became aware of some new technology that was being used in aircraft manufacturing around thermoplastics. So thermoformed plastics have a lot of advantages over composites, but also a lot of similarities. So it brings a lot of the same high gloss finish that you can get with composites, great strength to weight ratio, but it's a much cleaner and it's a much more, frankly, environmentally friendly process because the byproduct, the scrap plastic, you're able to recycle a lot easier than you are with composites, and you're working a lot fewer noxious chemicals than you are with some of the more composite fiberglass heavy production styles. So any line became aware of what was on in the aviation industry and started tooling around with it first with seats and combinings and bullheads, small parts switching that from fiberglass over to thermoplastics. The big goal on the horizon was obviously the deck and the hole, the main components of the craft, and eventually Edline was able to nail that. And so we became the first to thermoform a boat in the mid nineties, and over the course of the next decade, we switched all of our manufacturing from composite boats over to Thermoform.", "token_estimate": 515, "speakers": ["Scott Holley", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "04:11", "end_timestamp": "04:41", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 55: Scott Holley | On Water Marketing | Eddyline Kayak", "slug": "ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "guest_name": "Scott Holley", "guest_title": "President, Eddyline Kayaks", "categories": [], "short_description": "User Group Guerrilla Style Marketing", "podcast_length": "44:77"}, {"id": "869d5856-4d74-409a-a28d-3f29c1454321", "episode_id": "ee39605a-3ae6-4fee-b613-c613abdb9110", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485d", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (06:35): What are some of the main benefits to that method, I guess? Why do you do that? It sounds like it's more complicated or a little more challenging.\n\nScott Holley (06:45): It has some unique technical challenges. There's not a lot of technical challenges in thermoforming per se, but in terms of the part size that we're working with, there's very few applications where you're thermoforming a 16 foot part. (07:00): Obviously. You've got to make sure not only that it's visually appealing, but that it has great structural integrity as well dimensional stability while paddling year in year out with different thermal conditions across the material. And then scratch resistance, impact resistance. We spent a lot of time working with a lot of different plastic suppliers to come up with the right formulation, a plastic part that works for us. It's a part that we have trade named carbon light, and really that's to evoke the similarities that it has with carbon fiber technology in terms of appearance, strength to weight ratio, dimensional stability and impact resistance.\n\nCole Heilborn (07:47): Oh, interesting. So what do you, I guess why not use carbon then? What's the downside?\n\nScott Holley (07:54): The downside is it takes about 10 times as long per boat and all of the extra scrap ends up in a landfill instead of recycled.\n\nCole Heilborn (08:03): Gotcha. Okay.\n\nScott Holley (08:04): And for, it's not nearly as clean for the employees to work with as well, so Well, you've been here out to our shop here, we've got a very clean shop, a very efficient shop, and not a lot of byproducts that are harmful to our team, which is really important to us.\n\nCole Heilborn (08:19): And I guess why manufacturer here? You could go anywhere.\n\nScott Holley (08:25): Yeah, we could certainly, there's some disadvantages to manufacturing in the extreme upper left corner of the country. We sell a lot of kayaks down in Florida, and that's a heck of a long journey for those boats to make it from Burlington, Washington to Tampa Bay, Florida, for instance.\n\nCole Heilborn (08:49): I mean, you only have a couple mountain ranges.", "token_estimate": 537, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Scott Holley"], "start_timestamp": "06:35", "end_timestamp": "08:49", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 55: Scott Holley | On Water Marketing | Eddyline Kayak", "slug": "ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "guest_name": "Scott Holley", "guest_title": "President, Eddyline Kayaks", "categories": [], "short_description": "User Group Guerrilla Style Marketing", "podcast_length": "44:77"}, {"id": "2f0ed84e-7518-468c-b35b-28aea6edadfb", "episode_id": "ee39605a-3ae6-4fee-b613-c613abdb9110", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485d", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Scott Holley (08:51): Yeah, a few mountain ranges in between. Yeah, exactly. So I'll talk about the advantages to having all of our operations under one roof, and then we'll talk about the specific advantages to the Pacific Northwest (09:05): By having all of our back office, front office and production functions all under the same roof. It allows for very tight integration, which really shows itself when you're talking about the service levels that we're able to deliver to our end user customers and to our dealers. So production and sales, hand and glove, they always know what the other party's doing because we're interacting day to day. If I were to look out over my shoulder right now, I could tell you exactly what boats are being manufactured for who. And if you're talking about a model where you have, for instance, overseas manufacturing, you're taking that a little bit more in faith and you don't have the same level of real-time information. (09:52): So we're able to maintain a pretty diverse product line with a lot of different color options. In normal times. We have both preseason and ASAP deliveries where we're able to meet and respond to in-season demand. And that's just a challenging thing to do if you don't have all of your functions working under the roof together. So that's why we think it's important to have an integrated company in terms of why we're here in the Pacific Northwest, we're here because this is where we started and this is where we grew up, but we also, we have team members who have been here 20 and 25 years, and that level of knowledge skill is something that is not easily replaced or replicated.\n\nSpeaker 3 (10:41): So\n\nScott Holley (10:42): It would be challenging to pick up all of we, you could put a dollar figure on picking up the equipment and moving it somewhere else. But the other elements of the business, the institutional knowledge, the culture, the way the team works together, that's a little bit tougher to do, but it's also just, it's a great place to be for our end users. We're paddlers ourselves. We love getting out on the water. Again, lots of options here. And so yeah, we love it.", "token_estimate": 540, "speakers": ["Scott Holley", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "08:51", "end_timestamp": "10:42", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 55: Scott Holley | On Water Marketing | Eddyline Kayak", "slug": "ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "guest_name": "Scott Holley", "guest_title": "President, Eddyline Kayaks", "categories": [], "short_description": "User Group Guerrilla Style Marketing", "podcast_length": "44:77"}, {"id": "c2aa657d-9a09-401c-ae04-02708d4fa5a4", "episode_id": "ee39605a-3ae6-4fee-b613-c613abdb9110", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485d", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole Heilborn (11:14): Not to mention some great product testing opportunities.\n\nScott Holley (11:17): Oh yeah. Every chance we get.\n\nCole Heilborn (11:19): Yeah. So how, I guess, would it be more cost effective to, for example, go overseas per boat?\n\nScott Holley (11:28): I don't think so. It's not something that we have any intention of looking at, but just to the back of the envelope, if you're talking about the delays of shipping something on a boat, loading kayaks onto a container and shipping it across Pacific, again, you really lose that responsiveness to demand where you're not able to turn around a product order as quickly. With as much variability, you get more shipping damage. And for us, our ability to innovate really relies upon that tight integration between sales and production. So that's something that we would lose as well.\n\nCole Heilborn (12:13): Yeah. When did the company start? I don't know if we caught that.\n\nScott Holley (12:17): Yeah, Eddie Line was founded in 1971. This is our 50th anniversary, and Tom Dare, the founder, retired just four and a half years ago.\n\nCole Heilborn (12:28): Okay.\n\nScott Holley (12:29): 50-year-old company on its second ownership team, but has been family owned and operated for the continuity of its company's existence.\n\nCole Heilborn (12:39): Sure. So then when did you join the company?", "token_estimate": 340, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Scott Holley"], "start_timestamp": "11:14", "end_timestamp": "12:39", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 55: Scott Holley | On Water Marketing | Eddyline Kayak", "slug": "ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "guest_name": "Scott Holley", "guest_title": "President, Eddyline Kayaks", "categories": [], "short_description": "User Group Guerrilla Style Marketing", "podcast_length": "44:77"}, {"id": "4f2f3442-bf0d-4132-95c0-cc0051a5cb51", "episode_id": "ee39605a-3ae6-4fee-b613-c613abdb9110", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485d", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Scott Holley (12:42): Yeah, so I joined about four and a half years ago, summer of 2017. I had a background that was not in kayak manufacturing. It's a little bit different. It's more investment banking, consulting type work. And I was looking to leave that industry and join a company that I could have more of a hands-on approach with. (13:04): So I left the banking industry in 2016, and I lived in the area, I was living in the area, started looking for a company that I could help buy, and I came across Eddie Line, met with Tom De and his wife, Lisa Dere, who were, again, 46 years in. At that point, they were starting to think about retirement, and so it was good timing. I met the management team who had really been running the company on a day-to-day basis for quite a while, and we really hit it off. There's a lot of mutual respect. And then I think a lot of great ideas that were in alignment about what we could do with the company. So what we ended up doing was we got together and we put an offer and we bought the company from Tom and Lisa so that they were able to retire and have an exit on their ownership in the company. And we picked up and day one, we stood in front of the crew and said, this is what happened. We bought the company from Tom and Lisa. They're retiring, and really nothing changes. We're going to continue to do what we've been doing, which is manufacturing high quality craft with quality designs and a focus on service and support. (14:29): So that's promise that we made to the crew, and that's what we've been trying to live up to every day since.\n\nCole Heilborn (14:35): Yeah. And why the move for yourself? You said you wanted to do something a little more, what was the word you used? Hands-on? Tangible. Yeah. Yeah. Investment banking to", "token_estimate": 447, "speakers": ["Scott Holley", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "12:42", "end_timestamp": "14:35", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 55: Scott Holley | On Water Marketing | Eddyline Kayak", "slug": "ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "guest_name": "Scott Holley", "guest_title": "President, Eddyline Kayaks", "categories": [], "short_description": "User Group Guerrilla Style Marketing", "podcast_length": "44:77"}, {"id": "704a9407-65f3-4582-87d3-3cf057af2be1", "episode_id": "ee39605a-3ae6-4fee-b613-c613abdb9110", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485d", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Scott Holley (14:47): Kayaks. It's a bit of a jump. I remember the first time I was sitting down in an office with Tom De and he asked me what my kayaking experience was, and I had been kayaking a handful of times, but the last thing I was going to do is look at one of the godfathers of the industry and say anything other than, I'm an amateur, I've got nothing. What I told him at the time was I wasn't looking for a great company to buy that I could have a hand in helping to take it to the next level. And he told me afterwards that that was the right answer. They were looking for someone who would help preserve the legacy, and importantly help preserve the jobs of the people who had trusted their livelihoods with the company. And so luckily, we get to have the best of both worlds. It's a great company with a lot of opportunities that we've been able to take advantage of on the business side, but it's also a product that I just enjoy the heck out of, like you said, product testing. I've got my kayak on my roof rack pretty much all the time, and I'm out by myself or with friends or with family any chance I get. And it's just an amazing, wonderful sport.\n\nCole Heilborn (16:10): So in terms of taking the company to the next level, what does that mean for you guys?", "token_estimate": 320, "speakers": ["Scott Holley", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "14:47", "end_timestamp": "16:10", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 55: Scott Holley | On Water Marketing | Eddyline Kayak", "slug": "ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "guest_name": "Scott Holley", "guest_title": "President, Eddyline Kayaks", "categories": [], "short_description": "User Group Guerrilla Style Marketing", "podcast_length": "44:77"}, {"id": "dabaf1c9-0d3d-4f22-bf05-639a1e2d5941", "episode_id": "ee39605a-3ae6-4fee-b613-c613abdb9110", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485d", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Scott Holley (16:17): Yeah, so Eddie Lyon's 50 years old, but we're still a growth company, so that's a really interesting combination. We've been growing our production and sales for the last 12 years, year over year increases, and that's at least up until last year was against a backdrop of an industry that was kind of flat, (16:37): I would say. So we recognized when we were going through the process of buying the company that we're really in a pretty awesome position. If I were to break out the paddle sport marketplace, specifically within Stillwater Kayaks, you still have the premium high-end composite boats that absolutely have a place, and there's some amazing designs, amazing crafts out there at higher price points as they should be. But the volumes there are quite a bit lower than what they were in the eighties or nineties, for instance. And then you've got a lot of price point boats, a lot of rotomolded, polyethylene boats that are principally sold through big box stores, and those volumes have exploded over the last few years and the prices on those, it's really been a bit of a race to the bottom on the pricing side, but that has the advantage of getting a lot of people into the sports. So we recognize the advantage that that has, and we're right there in the middle where we're able, again, with our manufacturing process and our designs to replicate a lot of the form function benefits that you get with the top end boats at a fraction of the price. (17:56): And we have, for the people who have entered the industry with a price point boat, maybe they bought it at one of the big box stores that have gotten into the sport, they love it and they're looking to upgrade to a sleeker, more efficient, lighter craft. We're a natural step in that progression for them. So it's a really cool place to be in the industry as participation rates have increased and people have gotten their feet wet to torture, pun there, we're able to give them a really great paddling experience for their next boat. And so we've seen a lot of growth come through that.", "token_estimate": 520, "speakers": ["Scott Holley"], "start_timestamp": "16:17", "end_timestamp": "16:17", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 55: Scott Holley | On Water Marketing | Eddyline Kayak", "slug": "ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "guest_name": "Scott Holley", "guest_title": "President, Eddyline Kayaks", "categories": [], "short_description": "User Group Guerrilla Style Marketing", "podcast_length": "44:77"}, {"id": "7d1945f0-ac8b-475c-a925-b158e38d375c", "episode_id": "ee39605a-3ae6-4fee-b613-c613abdb9110", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485d", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Cole Heilborn (18:35): Gotcha. So next level means just are you guys working on developing new product, refining, design, increasing revenue, I guess all of the above?\n\nScott Holley (18:47): Yeah, absolutely. All of the above. First and foremost, it's staying grounded to our core mission and purpose, which is to make sure that people are able to paddle clean and healthy waterways in a safe and equitable way. So we're really trying to be good citizens, good stewards of the industry and that perspective. So safety and design is first and foremost, it was prior to the acquisition, and it is what we live every day. We definitely have been focused on product design and development. We haven't it shrunk back from our duties there at all. In fact, we've really ramped up our research and development since we've made the acquisition, (19:32): And I'd really put r and d into a couple of different buckets. There's the iterative, slight tweaks and changes, filling out gaps in the product line, and then there's kind of the bigger leaps. So going from composite to thermoforming was a big leap, and we're keeping our eye on the horizon, looking at materials, looking at different things that we could be doing to make sure that we're constantly thinking about what that next big leap might be. And in the meantime, we've really been looking at holes in the product line, starting with the paddler and their experience and their needs, and seeing how we can make a more intuitive experience that really does provide that access to a broad range of paddlers, paddling types, body types, paddling styles. So in our first couple of years we released, we had turnover about 30% of our lineup. We introduced four new boats and retired a couple as well. (20:40): And that was really in that second group of innovation that I mentioned with the iterative designs. So we moved from unique nomenclature. Every boat has its own name to more of a family type approach of boats. So for instance, we introduced a day touring boat and called the day touring line, the Sitka line. We have three models, St. Lt, LT, xt. So it's pretty self descriptive when you're looking at those three boats that you're talking about, three boats that share common characteristics of whole design, use case, et cetera, but that are designed for three different paddler sizes.", "token_estimate": 587, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Scott Holley"], "start_timestamp": "18:35", "end_timestamp": "18:47", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 55: Scott Holley | On Water Marketing | Eddyline Kayak", "slug": "ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "guest_name": "Scott Holley", "guest_title": "President, Eddyline Kayaks", "categories": [], "short_description": "User Group Guerrilla Style Marketing", "podcast_length": "44:77"}, {"id": "89c967ce-ff0f-44a3-bfed-cc7dcea7045e", "episode_id": "ee39605a-3ae6-4fee-b613-c613abdb9110", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485d", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Cole Heilborn (21:20): I see.\n\nScott Holley (21:22): So that's what we've been working on. We've got a bunch of other stuff that is more skunkwork type stuff that we're super excited about. Unfortunately, I'm going to just have to say stay tuned with that.\n\nCole Heilborn (21:33): Fair enough. We're used to that answer on this show,\n\nScott Holley (21:36): I'm sure. Well, I'll tell you what, Cole, I'll give you a little bit of a scoop when that's coming out and we will reconvene if you're interested.\n\nCole Heilborn (21:44): Perfect. So something that we chatted about a few weeks ago is just the community, I think that Eddie Line has and the community and how that reflects in product design, product feedback, but also in terms of you guys market, how you choose to market, but also how you choose not to market. And I'm curious if you can kind of break that down for us and what that looks like.", "token_estimate": 226, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Scott Holley"], "start_timestamp": "21:20", "end_timestamp": "21:44", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 55: Scott Holley | On Water Marketing | Eddyline Kayak", "slug": "ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "guest_name": "Scott Holley", "guest_title": "President, Eddyline Kayaks", "categories": [], "short_description": "User Group Guerrilla Style Marketing", "podcast_length": "44:77"}, {"id": "f32de17a-eb58-4eae-8365-90c68330b4ef", "episode_id": "ee39605a-3ae6-4fee-b613-c613abdb9110", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485d", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Scott Holley (22:16): Yeah, absolutely. One of the most rewarding things about working here at Edline is having the privilege and opportunity of interacting with our paddling community and our dealer community. They're just an incredible group of people and I dunno how else to put it. The values of people who choose to spend their dollars in their time in the silent sports paddling, near shorelines, being close to nature. There's just a lot of shared values that that community has with the ownership team, an appreciation for nature, environmentally conscious, and just people who are really wonderful to spend time with. I know you've had a few podcasts lately on social media and its challenges, and I share those, and I appreciate that you've brought those up. I will say that one of the silver linings to social media for me has been people talking about and sharing the experiences that they've had in our product. So when someone buys an Eddie line and takes it out on the water with their father, their mother, their son, their best friend, and they snap a picture or take a video and they share it, they're giving us a glimpse into really what the best of the human experience is and life-defining moments. (23:42): And it really is just incredibly special that we're invited to be part of that journey with them, and it's a reminder of how important the stewardship of this company and this brand and these products are. But yeah, it's awesome. It's so cool to get calls and notes and emails the old fashioned way in addition to the tweets and the Instagram posts where we're seeing people have these life-changing experiences with our products. It really is a true privilege.\n\nCole Heilborn (24:17): So I feel like a common question for any marketer these days is what are all the ways that you're marketing? And I feel like maybe a good question for you guys are what are all the ways that you're not marketing and why? Because I feel like I got the sense that you guys don't do a whole lot in terms of the tons of digital, tons of SEO or fill in the blank. I guess, what are the things that you don't do and why?", "token_estimate": 533, "speakers": ["Scott Holley", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "22:16", "end_timestamp": "24:17", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 55: Scott Holley | On Water Marketing | Eddyline Kayak", "slug": "ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "guest_name": "Scott Holley", "guest_title": "President, Eddyline Kayaks", "categories": [], "short_description": "User Group Guerrilla Style Marketing", "podcast_length": "44:77"}, {"id": "776a6fb4-87ce-4cdf-9221-77e37358f69c", "episode_id": "ee39605a-3ae6-4fee-b613-c613abdb9110", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485d", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Scott Holley (24:45): Yeah, so for us of marketing, it really is part of the stool, which includes sales and product development, and it's all about how we interact with our customers. You're right. There's a lot of things that we don't do and that we haven't needed to do. If I back up in terms of over the last decade as a company I mentioned we're a 50-year-old company that's in a growth mode. We're in a very fortunate position where the end consumer demand and the dealer demand for our product has outpaced our ability to service it because of production capacity constraints, and because we're making a premium product that has a high level of craftsmanship in it, it really does limit our ability to kind of exponentially grow the product line because we're not just able to go out and hire anybody and have them immediately trained up on making the product. We're not able to go out and buy a lot of equipment and have robots running 24 7 to make the product either. So that puts some growth on, puts some constraints on our growth, which I think has been a tremendously helpful thing for the company (26:02): Because it makes sure that we're not straying too far from our roots and being very deliberate in how we grow and not just taking growth each year for growth's sake, but because the end consumer demand has been higher than our ability to produce the traditional marketing funnel of how do we get more people in the top, and then how do we progress them from awareness through a by decision all the way through to becoming an advocate of the product after the fact. Frankly, it would not be servicing the company to grow the demand faster than it is currently organically and create even more challenges in getting products to customers than we already have in terms of what that means in activities perspective and how we've invested. When we took over as a management owned company a few years ago, the marketing activities were fairly limited and they were also rather traditional. So producing a print catalog that independent retailers could hand out to customers getting an advertisement in the print circular for trade magazines, attending multiple trade shows. So I will say that we have made some forays to adjust to a more traditional marketing structure over the last few years, and we've got some ways to go. But at the end of the day, our goal for marketing really is not to create more awareness or generate more interest to buy, but it is more along the lines of education and support. (27:47): So we want our messages to go to people who have made a buy decision but want to have the best experience on the water so that they're aware of our online store, the offerings that are there. They're aware of the community of paddlers that exist, that they make the correct buy decision and that they don't just buy an Eddie line, but that they buy the right boat for them. We have the FIT acronym for you intended use type of water. It's not a quick and easy questionnaire that you answer yes and no and out spits the correct model on the backend, but it's more a dialogue that we invite users to engage in with themselves, with our friends who paddle and importantly with their independent retailers so that they can get in the correct boat and that they're well set for a good experience thereafter. And then frankly, the rest of the funnel in terms of creating advocates, it blows my mind how many passionate and informed Eddie Line advocates that we have out there that are doing that work without prompting. So our goal is really to make sure that they have the tools and the information to do that, but we have a lot of product evangelists that are out there, and certainly it's a large product. Anytime it's driving down the road, it's visible when it's out on the water, it's visible. There's a lot of conversations that are not just happening in Facebook groups, but in in-person paddling forums and side of the road when people are filling up gas tanks and they've got a netty line on the roof rack.", "token_estimate": 1010, "speakers": ["Scott Holley"], "start_timestamp": "24:45", "end_timestamp": "24:45", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 55: Scott Holley | On Water Marketing | Eddyline Kayak", "slug": "ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "guest_name": "Scott Holley", "guest_title": "President, Eddyline Kayaks", "categories": [], "short_description": "User Group Guerrilla Style Marketing", "podcast_length": "44:77"}, {"id": "999fe21a-4c09-4314-bc9a-9e6c463f61ac", "episode_id": "ee39605a-3ae6-4fee-b613-c613abdb9110", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485d", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Cole Heilborn (29:23): Sure, man, I feel like that sounds like an awesome position to be in. You've got so much demand that you can't keep up with it. A couple of questions. One, how backed up is your production cycle?\n\nScott Holley (29:40): Well, it's an interesting time to be asking that question. We're recording this January, 2021. So yeah, you've had a few guests who have talked about what's happened over the last year, but I'll just kind of back up and replay that from the paddle sports perspective, particularly as a Washington manufacturer, we were the first state to get shut down by COVID. We had the first known COVID case about 30 miles south of us about a year ago. And so last March, we shut down our manufacturing when our state shut down. And at the time we had built up quite a bit of inventory for spring shipments to our dealers that had bought on preseason, and at that point, no one knew what was going to happen. So we sent our crew home, turn off the lights in the facility. We did do a few things around bringing the crew back to help create personal protective equipment, which something we're super proud of that we were able to be one of many, many manufacturers who were able to step up in that way. (30:47): But otherwise we weren't producing boats, and we frankly had no idea if the demand was going to come back or not. Fast forward just a few weeks to Memorial Day, and it instantly became aware that people who had been cooped up inside who weren't able to get on a plane and go to Disneyland, were looking for ways to recreate in their backyard while maintaining six feet of social distance. And at our boat is 12 feet long and a paddle is about six feet long. We're sitting on the sport that was designed for the social distance era because we're such a relatively small industry compared to some of the service industries like tourism. Just that little bit of spend shift going from the plane trip to Disneyland into hard goods has created massive supply chain issues across the board. Again, one of the advantages of being a privately held company was we actually had made the decision in January to increase our inventory levels in anticipation of supply shortages. So we were able to get back in and really not miss a beat and get up to speed to meet the demand as best we could. (32:09): That said, we did sell out pretty quickly and we remain sold out. Our production is spoken for through the rest of the summer. Wow. At the moment, we're continuing to accelerate the growth of our production, I should say. We took occupancy of the new building a year ago, just a handful of weeks before we got shut down. That was the building call that you were able to take a walk through. So we were pretty fortunate with that timing that we're able to have increased production year over year, but it is still behind what the ultimate demand for our product is.", "token_estimate": 724, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Scott Holley"], "start_timestamp": "29:23", "end_timestamp": "29:40", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 55: Scott Holley | On Water Marketing | Eddyline Kayak", "slug": "ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "guest_name": "Scott Holley", "guest_title": "President, Eddyline Kayaks", "categories": [], "short_description": "User Group Guerrilla Style Marketing", "podcast_length": "44:77"}, {"id": "4c5ad3e0-eb8c-4b92-b2e1-d696ea5143db", "episode_id": "ee39605a-3ae6-4fee-b613-c613abdb9110", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485d", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Cole Heilborn (32:46): Gotcha. So some people would look at that and say, that's a bunch of missed opportunity. You've got six months of customers waiting to get their products, and then just imagine what you could do if you started to market and try to increase that awareness, increase that interest. Obviously, COVID brought on some unexpected production challenges, but it sounds like you're kind of happy with that few month forecast of production. I guess coming back to that, why, because you could do more.", "token_estimate": 129, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "32:46", "end_timestamp": "32:46", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 55: Scott Holley | On Water Marketing | Eddyline Kayak", "slug": "ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "guest_name": "Scott Holley", "guest_title": "President, Eddyline Kayaks", "categories": [], "short_description": "User Group Guerrilla Style Marketing", "podcast_length": "44:77"}, {"id": "2e7e2d4c-6262-45d9-8fe7-537031cb9917", "episode_id": "ee39605a-3ae6-4fee-b613-c613abdb9110", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485d", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Scott Holley (33:23): We could do more. Well, it really starts with the alignment that happens when you're management owners. We could potentially do more. We could let our quality slip, we could higher up without regards to capability. We could tell people to spend less time making sure that the boat looks nice on the backend and have one year of phenomenal sails. And in the process put a black mark on what is a 50 year brand recognition for quality. So at the end of the day, we're just not willing to do that. The upside of letting our core values slip for a while to go and do a quick cash grab and end up at the end of the day with an impaired brand is as owners, something that just doesn't serve us. If we were getting a call from a corporate office in some far off place telling us to keep the machines running 24 7 and go and hire any warm body to fill a station to keep the line moving, and by the way, it's just a production bonus. So that's what your incentive is that I'd like to think that the type of people who wouldn't do that anyway, but (34:52): Incentives do matter. And in our case, it's great to be in a position where myself and my two manager partners who are here, we're stewards of something super special that we really believe in. And at the end of the day, that's more important to us than leaving a few orders on the table. The biggest challenge with leaving orders on the table isn't the fact that it represents dollars to us, but it's more that it represents dollars to our dealers. So when we think about the criticality of ramping up production in a smart and safe way, it's really to make sure that we're able to meet the needs and requests of our dealer customers because we want them to be successful. And that successful means having product on the shelf and in stock when the getting's good.\n\nCole Heilborn (35:48): Yeah. Do you have any idea of if I found a new customer, but I'm considering getting into kayaking and I'm looking at Eddie Line and I go to, I don't know your website, and I see, oh, I have to wait six months or whatever to get a kayak, do you have any idea of how many people you're losing because of that backup?", "token_estimate": 544, "speakers": ["Scott Holley", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "33:23", "end_timestamp": "35:48", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 55: Scott Holley | On Water Marketing | Eddyline Kayak", "slug": "ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "guest_name": "Scott Holley", "guest_title": "President, Eddyline Kayaks", "categories": [], "short_description": "User Group Guerrilla Style Marketing", "podcast_length": "44:77"}, {"id": "4dd0cceb-f7b3-4bf5-89e7-e1c8e88882cc", "episode_id": "ee39605a-3ae6-4fee-b613-c613abdb9110", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485d", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Scott Holley (36:11): Not as many as I would think. And it's possible that folks that we're losing are choosing to pick up another brand and another product, and that's the way it goes. But I am constantly shocked to see, again, social media posts from people who driving 500 miles in the dead of winter because a retailer, the next state over has the boat that they've been waiting for six months. And again, those customer evangelists on the back end, the people who are believers in the product and who are helping shepherd their paddlers towards our brand are reinforcing that message that it's worth the wait. And so we're grateful to be in that position and to be a brand who's looked upon that way.\n\nCole Heilborn (37:02): I'm curious to learn more about, I guess, how you guys create that type of loyalty. A customer driving 500 miles to go pick up an Eddie line and they've had to wait six months for something they've never used before is pretty incredible. How do you do that?", "token_estimate": 247, "speakers": ["Scott Holley", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "36:11", "end_timestamp": "37:02", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 55: Scott Holley | On Water Marketing | Eddyline Kayak", "slug": "ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "guest_name": "Scott Holley", "guest_title": "President, Eddyline Kayaks", "categories": [], "short_description": "User Group Guerrilla Style Marketing", "podcast_length": "44:77"}, {"id": "0cf3615e-120c-4d35-9045-9e72acca3238", "episode_id": "ee39605a-3ae6-4fee-b613-c613abdb9110", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485d", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Scott Holley (37:21): It really starts with the legacy that we inherited When we bought the company. The company was founded by people for whom business was a bit of a four letter word. Tom and Lisa Dara were self-described hippies in the seventies who got into business by accident because they made a kayak for themselves and then made one from a friend or for a friend. Story old is time, right? All of a sudden, you've got a storefront and a list of customers handing you money, and all of a sudden you're a business owner. But for them, it always started with the boat. It was, how can we make a better product? How can we make sure that it's higher quality? What can we do that's next that will improve the experience of the paddler? For them, it was for themselves. How can we make a better paddling boat that would work for us? And because that's where they chose to invest, we inherited a company that for 45 years had invested in product design, product quality that hadn't invested in as much in marketing and advertising as was the story told itself in the product. So I think that's something that has a level of authenticity that is hard to create, doesn't get created overnight, and if you're not careful, can go away if you don't continue with that legacy. (38:53): So while we are investing in both traditional marketing as well as some new digital marketing initiatives I can talk about, we really are continuing to say, all right, from an investment perspective, how are we making sure that we're designing products that are meeting our customer's needs and are continuing to innovate and move to what's next?\n\nCole Heilborn (39:18): Man, that's good. I think you just summed it up right there. The whole idea is the best form of marketing is the best quality product.\n\nScott Holley (39:27): That's it.\n\nCole Heilborn (39:28): So talk a little more about some of the digital marketing that you guys are starting and looking into.", "token_estimate": 487, "speakers": ["Scott Holley", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "37:21", "end_timestamp": "39:28", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 55: Scott Holley | On Water Marketing | Eddyline Kayak", "slug": "ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "guest_name": "Scott Holley", "guest_title": "President, Eddyline Kayaks", "categories": [], "short_description": "User Group Guerrilla Style Marketing", "podcast_length": "44:77"}, {"id": "fea6e701-dce6-4986-83d2-8f8fce122a17", "episode_id": "ee39605a-3ae6-4fee-b613-c613abdb9110", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485d", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Scott Holley (39:35): Yeah, so about a year ago when we were shut down, we were, I think like a lot of people, we had the time, since we weren't making product to be thinking a lot about what our company would be and what our company would need to survive in a period of uncertain demand. After the fact, and this was before we knew that paddle sports were going to blow up in a good way. So we made the decision at that point to accelerate our digital marketing efforts. And even though it turns out that we didn't need to go out and create demand and widen a funnel, we made a lot of investments that have helped us in other ways. (40:19): We ended up contracting with an agency called Blink out of Canada. They've done great work for us. I know that there's a number of great agencies out there. I know that you're affiliated with One Coal, and there's a lot of great groups out there. But for us, it just made a lot of sense rather than trying to build a broad array of capabilities in-house to find a group that had a SEO person, creative person, social copy, technical capability for website development. And we had them build us a website, which was we went from WordPress to a Shopify site. We wanted to have a clean experience across phone, tablet, browser, and really make sure that the quality of the imagery that we were presenting in the digital world was matching the quality of experience that people were getting when they walked into a showroom. So we had that new website go live in December of this year. (41:39): For the last few months, we've also been working with this agency on the social side and really focused on user generated content. We like most brands will go out and we'll do professional shoots, video stills, we will do on products, hang tags, et cetera, and we really want to make sure that we have those professional images. But in terms of helping people imagine themselves in a boat, there's nothing quite like the user content out there where authentic smiles, different water types, different paddler types, different boats, and the stories that accompany it. I know a lot of your guests have talked about the importance of storytelling. Every paddler has a story. Every paddler is a story. The story of that particular outing, the story of how they got into the sport, the story of what that sport means to them, and there really is no shortcut to telling stories when you're talking about your paddling community other than investing time and effort into listening, understanding, curating, and then from a very consent driven perspective, telling those stories to the general public. That's what we've really been focused on the social media side, and I'm really thrilled with the work that our group's done in being able to bring those stories to light, letting people see themselves in the position of the paddler that they might become.", "token_estimate": 721, "speakers": ["Scott Holley"], "start_timestamp": "39:35", "end_timestamp": "39:35", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 55: Scott Holley | On Water Marketing | Eddyline Kayak", "slug": "ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "guest_name": "Scott Holley", "guest_title": "President, Eddyline Kayaks", "categories": [], "short_description": "User Group Guerrilla Style Marketing", "podcast_length": "44:77"}, {"id": "94cbbda3-4cb8-4514-915b-ed5a4c13ce2a", "episode_id": "ee39605a-3ae6-4fee-b613-c613abdb9110", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485d", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Cole Heilborn (43:15): Yeah, it sounds like it just kind of creates another way for that community to exist and to thrive that's already there.\n\nScott Holley (43:22): Absolutely.\n\nCole Heilborn (43:23): Yeah. So you said you started that because of COVID and the uncertainty that come out of it. Oh, interesting. Interesting.\n\nScott Holley (43:32): Yeah. We always had a longer term plan, I think pretty much consistent with boardrooms management offices around the country where they looked at COVID and they looked at their five-year digital marketing strategy and said something like, okay, how can we do this in five months or five weeks? (43:53): And we were not unique in that, but that was the conversation we had. But it really is a benefit to us now because with the increased participation, we have brought in a new class of consumer over this past year, rather, who really has leapfrogged a lot of the steps that a typical customer would in terms of spending one or two seasons in a price point boat on easy water, and then upgrading to our boat and maybe taking a class and then a foray into sea kayaking. We've seen people who have just gone from zero to 60, and they're the sport with a lot less background and experience and skillset. They need a little bit more handholding from a digital perspective. Maybe their dealers at the outset was doing just curbside pickup classes in person become a little more problematic. So for us, getting that transmitting as much of that in-person experience through a digital format as possible is critical. And that's something that we're glad we made that investment. We're continuing to make that investment.\n\nCole Heilborn (45:00): What are some of the biggest challenges outside of the obvious production ones, I guess, what are the biggest growth areas that you look at and you say, yeah, we are doing good, but we could be doing better?", "token_estimate": 474, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Scott Holley"], "start_timestamp": "43:15", "end_timestamp": "45:00", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 55: Scott Holley | On Water Marketing | Eddyline Kayak", "slug": "ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "guest_name": "Scott Holley", "guest_title": "President, Eddyline Kayaks", "categories": [], "short_description": "User Group Guerrilla Style Marketing", "podcast_length": "44:77"}, {"id": "74707420-e9df-4904-81a5-af51ed94716b", "episode_id": "ee39605a-3ae6-4fee-b613-c613abdb9110", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485d", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Scott Holley (45:13): Yeah. I think for us, a lot of that's geographic. We just entered Canada a couple of years ago. We had to put a little bit of the breaks on that. I think we've got some geographic expansion opportunities. We've really been successful in growing off the backs of successful dealers. So you go down to Bend, Oregon in the summer and it looks like an Eline convention when you're pulling into the Deschutes River parking lot or up into Sparks Lakes. We just have so much penetration there. But that really began with Jeff and his crew over at Tlo Creek. They built a Whitewater Park, they built a Class Act retailer, and our growth has been synonymous with their growth and success because they do it really, really well. (46:09): And as we look out, there's a lot of communities that share a lot of similarities to bend, and you get these digital expats now with COVID where they're all working virtually and they've left San Francisco and moved to Bend or to Jackson Hole or to Bentonville or wherever they're going. All of a sudden, they have space and they want to get toys. So I think we've got a real opportunity to continue to replicate areas like that, working hand in glove with solid independent retailers to grow flat water kayaking as a sport in areas where it doesn't have as much of a following as it otherwise could or should.\n\nCole Heilborn (46:53): That's cool. I'm excited, I think, and I'm excited that you guys are just doing things a little differently. So much of what we talk about on this show is just some tactical stuff or specific things that people are doing or campaigns or brand managing or I don't know anything. And we've had a few guests on the show who have talked a lot about just about the importance of products, but it's always good to be reminded, I think, for anyone in this space, just that the product is, I mean, that's what you're selling, so it's everything, and ultimately you do it long enough, 50 years, and the community supports you. That's cool.", "token_estimate": 504, "speakers": ["Scott Holley", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "45:13", "end_timestamp": "46:53", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 55: Scott Holley | On Water Marketing | Eddyline Kayak", "slug": "ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "guest_name": "Scott Holley", "guest_title": "President, Eddyline Kayaks", "categories": [], "short_description": "User Group Guerrilla Style Marketing", "podcast_length": "44:77"}, {"id": "81e01b60-e2c6-4160-9a8a-f6043b83cf10", "episode_id": "ee39605a-3ae6-4fee-b613-c613abdb9110", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485d", "chunk_index": 21, "content": "Scott Holley (47:33): Yeah, absolutely. Cool. I'm so grateful for your podcast, and I've learned a ton from your tactical experts who have come on, and I'm not afraid to say that. I'm absolutely not one of them. So I appreciate the learning that's going on in the community that you're enabling, so thank you for that.\n\nCole Heilborn (47:49): Oh, absolutely. Again, I say this to every guest, but I'm happy to share the things that people are talking about and learning, but really, I just enjoy it myself because such a good educational process. And so I, it's been fun to have people on the show just from varying perspectives and industries and backgrounds and levels of experience, all kind of sharing what they're doing, and it's good to kind of see the whole picture rather than just focusing in on one particular element.\n\nScott Holley (48:20): Yeah, absolutely.\n\nCole Heilborn (48:21): If people want to follow along with you with Eddie Line, where can they go to connect with you?\n\nScott Holley (48:27): Yeah, we're pretty easy to find. Eddie Line, E-D-D-Y-L-I-N-E, Facebook, Instagram, Eddie line.com is our website, and my email isScott@eddieline.com. If anyone wants to drop me an email, happy to respond to anyone directly who has a thought or a question or wants to talk about ways to work together. We're always open to those as well.\n\nCole Heilborn (48:53): Perfect. Well, sounds good. Scott, thank you again for taking the time to chat, and I wish you guys the best for the next 50 years.\n\nScott Holley (49:02): Thanks. Really appreciate it, Cole.\n\nCole Heilborn (49:03): All right. Have a good day.\n\nScott Holley (49:05): You too.\n\nCole Heilborn (49:08): Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 471, "speakers": ["Scott Holley", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "47:33", "end_timestamp": "49:08", "podcast_index": 54, "episode_name": "Ep 55: Scott Holley | On Water Marketing | Eddyline Kayak", "slug": "ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-55-scott-holley-on-water-marketing-eddyline-kayak", "guest_name": "Scott Holley", "guest_title": "President, Eddyline Kayaks", "categories": [], "short_description": "User Group Guerrilla Style Marketing", "podcast_length": "44:77"}, {"id": "97d60c21-ad63-4649-9fc5-6c7aad4c0694", "episode_id": "f3d3877a-90df-4627-b5d1-33817e61af97", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4825", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:03): Welcome to Brand Story. My name is Cole Hilborn. Today I'm here with Tom and Kylie of Freedom Vans. How are you guys? Good.\n\nSpeaker 2 (00:10): Doing great.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:11): Alright. Well thanks for being here. We are here in Bellingham, Washington Freedom Vans. Really quickly tell me what you guys do. What are you all about?\n\nKyleigh (00:20): We are a van conversion company. We convert the big Mercedes Sprinter vans and Ford Transits into camper vans. So we do all of the interior bed solar power systems, kitchen galleys, et cetera.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:36): Perfect. I'm assuming that the name Freedom Vans kind of ties into what you guys are all about here.\n\nSpeaker 2 (00:41): Yeah, so Freedom Van's name came about from just the liberating experience of living the van life and that kind of all organically grew together from mountain biking and wanting to go places with a dog and just needing a place to stay that's easy to get what you need done. And so the name Freedom Vans really revolves around having freedom and Van Life definitely is centered around freedom. If you actually watch Serj KO's Van Life documentary, the End, everyone's asked to describe Van Life in one word, and nine out of 10 people say freedom. And so that resonated with me and it's not, I mean, that didn't cause me to choose the name, but I chose the name because for me, I had freedom when I lived out of a van and that was huge for me. So the name really resonated and that's what we called it.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:47): Awesome. It is pretty appropriate, totally. Van Life, certainly maybe because of Instagram, maybe not because of Instagram has become quite popular. It's become a certain aspire. People aspire to kind of live that on the road lifestyle. As people that have done it though, is it really all it's cracked up to be\n\nKyleigh (02:11): As it has its challenges, but it's a different pace of life. All of your chores and responsibilities are done in 30 minutes of your day. You do it a little bit more often, but", "token_estimate": 511, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Speaker 2", "Kyleigh"], "start_timestamp": "00:03", "end_timestamp": "02:11", "podcast_index": 5, "episode_name": "Ep 5: Tom and Kyleigh | How YouTube has Grown their Business | Freedom Vans", "slug": "ep-5-tom-and-kyleigh-how-youtube-has-grown-their-business-freedom-vans-92aa1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-5-tom-and-kyleigh-how-youtube-has-grown-their-business-freedom-vans-92aa1", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, I'm joined by Tom and Kyleigh and their two dogs from Freedom Vans. We discuss their origins, the realities of van life and how YouTube has doubled the waitlist on their production timelines.", "podcast_length": "20:09"}, {"id": "4b691f08-9e74-409e-9420-38c811643ac1", "episode_id": "f3d3877a-90df-4627-b5d1-33817e61af97", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4825", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Speaker 2 (02:23): Ultimately it really just depends on your goals and what you want to do with your life. And you don't have to living the van. Life doesn't mean you have to quit your job and plan this giant trip and go from Florida to Alaska and back\n\nSpeaker 4 (02:37): And\n\nSpeaker 2 (02:38): Down to Mexico. And really it's just about improving your day-to-day existence and being able to take your time and go on a trip that you can do the things that you want and sleep where you want to sleep comfortably. And yeah, just you have what you need to do what you want to do.\n\nKyleigh (03:06): I think it teaches you to appreciate life in a different way too. Have you read or heard of Marie life-changing Magic of Tidying Up?\n\nCole Heilborn (03:17): Yes. Yes, she is. She the person who if you have something and doesn't bring you joy,\n\nKyleigh (03:23): Get rid of it.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:24): Get rid of it, that's her. Okay.\n\nKyleigh (03:25): Yeah. So I feel like that's just such a good motto for life that I really started to understand once I moved into a van. Just getting rid of all my stuff with such a weight off my shoulders and downsizing to that level, I think has taught me a lot about what it means to be intentional. And that's just a good way of Van Life in general just really teaches you to be slower, more intentional with your time. One of our clients has the saying low miles, big smiles. I think it sums it up perfectly,", "token_estimate": 362, "speakers": ["Speaker 2", "Speaker 4", "Kyleigh", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "02:23", "end_timestamp": "03:25", "podcast_index": 5, "episode_name": "Ep 5: Tom and Kyleigh | How YouTube has Grown their Business | Freedom Vans", "slug": "ep-5-tom-and-kyleigh-how-youtube-has-grown-their-business-freedom-vans-92aa1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-5-tom-and-kyleigh-how-youtube-has-grown-their-business-freedom-vans-92aa1", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, I'm joined by Tom and Kyleigh and their two dogs from Freedom Vans. We discuss their origins, the realities of van life and how YouTube has doubled the waitlist on their production timelines.", "podcast_length": "20:09"}, {"id": "943b1ca5-2f62-4d25-a57a-d42fbba7a65e", "episode_id": "f3d3877a-90df-4627-b5d1-33817e61af97", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4825", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Speaker 2 (04:02): And I think what you're touching on is the Marie Kondo aspect is if you actually do commit to your living space, being in a van, you have limited space. So you've really got to prioritize what you want in your life and choose maybe two jackets and maybe two, three sets of pants and maybe two pairs of shoes. You got to consolidate and you don't have the space to let things pile up. And that really opens up a lot of room in your life for activities that you want to focus on and not so much chores, but more so freedom. And that's kind of opened up when you do downsize. Like tiny houses are an example of downsizing, but they're still 300 square feet, whereas in a van you've got 60 to a hundred square feet, and so you really got to narrow down your surroundings and your belongings and it really opens up room in your life.\n\nKyleigh (05:10): Just create space\n\nSpeaker 2 (05:11): To have the experience that you're after.\n\nCole Heilborn (05:14): Yeah, yeah. Well, that's awesome. So you guys recently have started a YouTube channel and you've been putting up videos of van walkthroughs. How has that gone? What's the result been of that? Great.\n\nKyleigh (05:32): I think we've had a really good,\n\nSpeaker 2 (05:35): I think it's been really great. So here's kind of how it breaks down for me. We always do a video right before the client picks up the vehicle (05:45): And is been approximately five to 600 hours of labor investment. And we do have a strong team of several builders that really all come together to make that happen. But I think that I've definitely just, I would get to the point where I'm so exhausted from building this van that by the time the end of the project comes around, I'm like, ah, I need a break. I need to go for a bike ride or something. And Kylie's super excited to get the YouTube video together and she brings this loving joy, loving joy and energy into her presentation. So usually she does the videos and I don't so much like, get me out of here. I need a break before the customer comes.", "token_estimate": 513, "speakers": ["Speaker 2", "Kyleigh", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "04:02", "end_timestamp": "05:35", "podcast_index": 5, "episode_name": "Ep 5: Tom and Kyleigh | How YouTube has Grown their Business | Freedom Vans", "slug": "ep-5-tom-and-kyleigh-how-youtube-has-grown-their-business-freedom-vans-92aa1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-5-tom-and-kyleigh-how-youtube-has-grown-their-business-freedom-vans-92aa1", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, I'm joined by Tom and Kyleigh and their two dogs from Freedom Vans. We discuss their origins, the realities of van life and how YouTube has doubled the waitlist on their production timelines.", "podcast_length": "20:09"}, {"id": "ae333b8b-5833-4f47-8087-6254df7e0113", "episode_id": "f3d3877a-90df-4627-b5d1-33817e61af97", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4825", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Kyleigh (06:38): It's interesting because I think that our YouTube channel and audience finds both of us really, it's really about building a relationship with our audience and showing people who we are, what we're about, what our company is about, and what we do and why we do it. It's such a good way to provide information and sometimes Tom feels like the community is just more receptive to women than men or want to seem more women than men.\n\nSpeaker 2 (07:12): I mean, I feel that's true. However, I think that male videos are very common and lots of people watch them, people describing things, and I think I can describe things, but I don't know.\n\nKyleigh (07:25): But as far as the clients that come to us, I feel like it's what Tom has to say in seeing his passion about what he does. When they can see that in a video, that's when they reach out to our company and come to us and they're like, wow, you guys are passionate about what you do. You like what you're doing? I want to work with you.\n\nCole Heilborn (07:45): So you've seen real business impact from the YouTube videos you do?\n\nKyleigh (07:50): Definitely. We went from maintaining a eight to 12 month wait list to having a, I mean, at this point we have an 18 month wait list.", "token_estimate": 312, "speakers": ["Kyleigh", "Speaker 2", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "06:38", "end_timestamp": "07:50", "podcast_index": 5, "episode_name": "Ep 5: Tom and Kyleigh | How YouTube has Grown their Business | Freedom Vans", "slug": "ep-5-tom-and-kyleigh-how-youtube-has-grown-their-business-freedom-vans-92aa1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-5-tom-and-kyleigh-how-youtube-has-grown-their-business-freedom-vans-92aa1", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, I'm joined by Tom and Kyleigh and their two dogs from Freedom Vans. We discuss their origins, the realities of van life and how YouTube has doubled the waitlist on their production timelines.", "podcast_length": "20:09"}, {"id": "fb4988c6-ac0e-4852-9b67-1ff6adcc791f", "episode_id": "f3d3877a-90df-4627-b5d1-33817e61af97", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4825", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Speaker 2 (08:04): Well, so the van tours are interesting in a lot of ways, and people are very interested to see what companies are doing as far as creativity, but it also serves as a relatively simple way of it. It's like a resume builder. (08:22): So we're putting these videos together at the end of a project, which we don't actually have finished vans for much longer than a couple weeks at most, unless they come back for some upgrades or whatever. So if we get a chance to showcase what we've done at a finished level, we take that opportunity through the YouTube videos and we get to show, so this was our vision and this is what we created and here's how to use it and here's how it works. And so that really helps clients feel like they know what they're going to get in the end, or they know what they can get and they can choose different spatial layouts and different finishes and just different things, but they know in the end it's going to work and they can trust us.\n\nCole Heilborn (09:20): Yeah,\n\nKyleigh (09:22): I've seen it help. The way that I see that YouTube's impacting our businesses from a marketing standpoint is also after we publish a YouTube video, we always have tons of emails and phone calls after the video's released.\n\nCole Heilborn (09:36): Interesting. Just people asking questions about the build, wanting to learn how they can do it, or all the above.\n\nKyleigh (09:43): I would say there are some questions. Most of the questions about how the build works or what components are going into it, things like that are usually people just comment directly on the YouTube. I think to take that extra step of reaching out to us via email or phone call, they're more interested. So we have noticed and the way we've gotten to a point in our business where we now have a form on our website that you fill out to contact us, so it helps us to kind of sort through,\n\nCole Heilborn (10:20): If that\n\nKyleigh (10:20): Makes sense.\n\nCole Heilborn (10:20): Yeah, no, that helps qualify,\n\nKyleigh (10:22): Right? Yeah. People who are talking to us are generally interested in the build and know what they're looking for. Yeah,", "token_estimate": 538, "speakers": ["Speaker 2", "Cole Heilborn", "Kyleigh"], "start_timestamp": "08:04", "end_timestamp": "10:22", "podcast_index": 5, "episode_name": "Ep 5: Tom and Kyleigh | How YouTube has Grown their Business | Freedom Vans", "slug": "ep-5-tom-and-kyleigh-how-youtube-has-grown-their-business-freedom-vans-92aa1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-5-tom-and-kyleigh-how-youtube-has-grown-their-business-freedom-vans-92aa1", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, I'm joined by Tom and Kyleigh and their two dogs from Freedom Vans. We discuss their origins, the realities of van life and how YouTube has doubled the waitlist on their production timelines.", "podcast_length": "20:09"}, {"id": "a3b8a77d-4211-4952-8d2b-db4015633da1", "episode_id": "f3d3877a-90df-4627-b5d1-33817e61af97", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4825", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole Heilborn (10:31): That wasn't one of us snoring. That was the dogs in the office. No, that's really cool. So I feel like you guys are kind of riding this wave of a few different trends. YouTube has been a popular thing, but it's always seems to be growing traction. Video itself in the last few years has grown to be incredibly popular, but then the business you're also in is in the van life, which again is a trend that's becoming more popular. It's been around for a while, but it seems to be really growing where, go ahead.\n\nKyleigh (11:04): I was going to say, people are kind of transitioning out of buying RVs, which you see all these big RV lots with tons of RVs to moving into a van, but it's such a small space where it's really hard to find everything you need in a van that's already built. So that's why I think a lot of people come to us because they can have something that's more attuned to the activities they're doing. We have a lot of mountain bikers, kayakers, a lot of more outdoor enthusiasts.\n\nCole Heilborn (11:33): Yeah, it's a different type of demographic who would get a van rather than an rv. Right,\n\nKyleigh (11:39): Totally.\n\nCole Heilborn (11:39): So you mentioned that a lot of what you do and the culture that you've built with your employees and your clients is just this family. Earlier you were telling me this story about one of your first clients who you didn't know personally. Tell us that origin story again. I think that really describes, I guess your guys' kind of approach to how you work with people and how it comes full circle.\n\nSpeaker 2 (12:03): So are we talking about Heidi?\n\nKyleigh (12:04): Heidi? Yep.\n\nSpeaker 2 (12:05): So in Colorado on, I was at the Enduro World Series in Crested Butte in 2015, and I was just going up this trail that was at 11, 12,000 feet, maybe 11,000 feet. And I started catching up to this lady who doesn't look so hot, and she's on the side of the trail and just tired, but holding\n\nKyleigh (12:33): There, it look so hot. Wasn't the nicest way to describe that. Well, she\n\nSpeaker 2 (12:37): Looked,\n\nCole Heilborn (12:37): She's a beautiful woman. She was so tired. Somehow", "token_estimate": 541, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Kyleigh", "Speaker 2"], "start_timestamp": "10:31", "end_timestamp": "12:37", "podcast_index": 5, "episode_name": "Ep 5: Tom and Kyleigh | How YouTube has Grown their Business | Freedom Vans", "slug": "ep-5-tom-and-kyleigh-how-youtube-has-grown-their-business-freedom-vans-92aa1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-5-tom-and-kyleigh-how-youtube-has-grown-their-business-freedom-vans-92aa1", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, I'm joined by Tom and Kyleigh and their two dogs from Freedom Vans. We discuss their origins, the realities of van life and how YouTube has doubled the waitlist on their production timelines.", "podcast_length": "20:09"}, {"id": "9302214d-4830-443e-91c4-ccbbd7749516", "episode_id": "f3d3877a-90df-4627-b5d1-33817e61af97", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4825", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Speaker 2 (12:40): She looked tired. Okay, sorry. So she looked tired and I just wanted to check on her and just give her whatever I could to try and help her. And I figured, oh, okay, maybe I can just offer her a hug. And so I offered her a hug and she accepted and I gave her a nice hug and she said she started to feel better after that. And I just did that out of the kindness of my heart, hoping that it would make her feel better. Then about a week or two later, we were over in Winter Park and Kylie was in the van in the parking lot, and she approached the van and started talking to Kylie about, oh, I'm looking for some help with my van, and I wanted to give solar power to people. And so she approached us after I met her that first time and\n\nKyleigh (13:40): Not knowing that it was Tom, she ran into me in the van and I started telling her about our van, what we were doing, and my boyfriend who wants to start, who's installing solar power systems for people.\n\nSpeaker 2 (13:55): And that was our goal, was to do solar for people. And then she said, yeah, I want solar. I had a battery die on me, and I did this night race. And so we were going to do solar and then we ended up doing walls and a bed platform. But it was great because we kind of started off on a nice note of a trusting, loving relationship. And then we maintained that relationship and she was letting us sleep at her house because we ended up working in her driveway, in her van. So she gave us her guest room. We were basically living with this woman having dinner with her every day. And it was like we created this really loving, friendly relationship.\n\nKyleigh (14:36): And all the interactions from that point work forward we're just so sweet and loving.\n\nCole Heilborn (14:41): Yeah, yeah. Well, so I think what you guys are describing is just, again, being authentic, being real people. How do you feel like those values, how do you feel like that's going to change marketing as we move into the future? Do you feel like there's changes that you're seeing that reflect those values\n\nKyleigh (15:03): Within our own company?", "token_estimate": 529, "speakers": ["Speaker 2", "Kyleigh", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "12:40", "end_timestamp": "15:03", "podcast_index": 5, "episode_name": "Ep 5: Tom and Kyleigh | How YouTube has Grown their Business | Freedom Vans", "slug": "ep-5-tom-and-kyleigh-how-youtube-has-grown-their-business-freedom-vans-92aa1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-5-tom-and-kyleigh-how-youtube-has-grown-their-business-freedom-vans-92aa1", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, I'm joined by Tom and Kyleigh and their two dogs from Freedom Vans. We discuss their origins, the realities of van life and how YouTube has doubled the waitlist on their production timelines.", "podcast_length": "20:09"}, {"id": "4c1698d9-39e9-4dc7-b54a-77934e48849e", "episode_id": "f3d3877a-90df-4627-b5d1-33817e61af97", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4825", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole Heilborn (15:04): Within your own company, but also just in the world of advertising and marketing as a whole?\n\nKyleigh (15:08): I wouldn't even say that this has been our marketing strategy. It's really just been us and it's been working, but as our team grows, we are less a part of the entire process than we used to be. We used to be with our clients. Every conversation from the start of start of their journey, when they reach out to us to the very end onwards, we still connect with them. Now we have Hannah, who's the front end, the first person they're meeting, Abby's helping with the design. Tom sometimes doesn't even really, I would say you meet all of our clients, but you're not quite as part as the process as you are just in the shop working with our team.\n\nSpeaker 2 (15:55): Yeah, I'm definitely more a part of the production side now than the client side. I mean, our whole journey is really driven by what the clients want. And now we have an engineer that works with the clients and I mean, everybody comes to us because they want or need a certain spatial layout that they can take wherever. So I'm more in production now.\n\nKyleigh (16:21): You're in production. So we are actually working on a YouTube video right now of meet the team so that people can see and build those relationships with the people who are behind their project,\n\nSpeaker 4 (16:41): Their\n\nKyleigh (16:41): Van, our work. And one thing that I see that I don't think that happens enough in marketing is people really building that personal relationship. Very few places have about pages, but I know for me, a lot of the times when I'm looking for a service or something, I'll go to about Page to see who I'll be interacting with. If there's someone that I think that I would get along well with. So I definitely think that companies should be doing that more building, and there is a way that you can do that on a larger scale. We're seeing ourselves. It's not about us connecting with the clients, but our company as a whole. How can we create that connection?", "token_estimate": 515, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Kyleigh", "Speaker 2", "Speaker 4"], "start_timestamp": "15:04", "end_timestamp": "16:41", "podcast_index": 5, "episode_name": "Ep 5: Tom and Kyleigh | How YouTube has Grown their Business | Freedom Vans", "slug": "ep-5-tom-and-kyleigh-how-youtube-has-grown-their-business-freedom-vans-92aa1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-5-tom-and-kyleigh-how-youtube-has-grown-their-business-freedom-vans-92aa1", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, I'm joined by Tom and Kyleigh and their two dogs from Freedom Vans. We discuss their origins, the realities of van life and how YouTube has doubled the waitlist on their production timelines.", "podcast_length": "20:09"}, {"id": "151afa3f-443a-4fec-8f7a-cef769866953", "episode_id": "f3d3877a-90df-4627-b5d1-33817e61af97", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4825", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (17:31): Yeah, yeah. No, well said. So you mentioned earlier the quality of life aspect. A lot of your clients are coming to you and they want this quality of life, I guess. Why is that something that people are looking for these days?\n\nKyleigh (17:48): Because our culture, so our culture is very work 40, and I think this is changing with the new generation, but just work long hours, build your retirement. We even get sucked into it. I think with Buy a house, do all these things, it's just we're constantly taking on more and more responsibility.\n\nSpeaker 4 (18:10): I\n\nThink it's so important to make space in your life to play (18:14): And to be able to do that in a way when you have a van. Mountain biking is the perfect example. If you want to go for a mountain bike trip, you don't have to worry about getting to destination a by this time to check in or to find a camping spot, et cetera. You can go at your own pace, be there whenever you want to be there, stop, ride a trail. Maybe that doesn't happen. So you switch up your whole plan and go to the next place and ride that trail. Take a nap in between, drink a beer, go ride another trail. Everything you need is just with you all the time, and it just creates so much more freedom. Of course, that word's just going to come up a lot and just room to play without any sort of agenda. And I think that we all just crave that just with how the Monday through Friday, nine to five life goes.\n\nCole Heilborn (19:12): Yeah, we want a little bit more, A little more adventure. A little more excitement sometimes,\n\nKyleigh (19:18): Right?\n\nCole Heilborn (19:18): Yeah,\n\nKyleigh (19:18): Totally.\n\nCole Heilborn (19:19): Yeah. Well, I know you guys have a lot of vans to be built, so I don't want to take up too much more of your time, but for folks that are listening, where can they find you guys?\n\nKyleigh (19:32): Our YouTube channel, which is Freedom Vans. I don't know if there's a direct link on YouTube,\n\nCole Heilborn (19:40): But\n\nKyleigh (19:41): If you search Freedom Vans, you'll find us. Okay. Our website, freedom vans.co Instagram. Really? If you just Google Freedom Vans, it's pretty easy to find us. You\n\nCole Heilborn (19:52): Guys, you'll populate. Alright, perfect. Well, I thank you guys for being on the show. It's been great getting to chat. But viewers, if you want to take a look at Freedom Fans, go ahead and check out their Instagram, YouTube, Facebook website. But thank you, Tom. Thank you Kylie, and we'll see you guys next time.", "token_estimate": 633, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Kyleigh", "Speaker 4", "Think it's so important to make space in your life to play"], "start_timestamp": "17:31", "end_timestamp": "19:52", "podcast_index": 5, "episode_name": "Ep 5: Tom and Kyleigh | How YouTube has Grown their Business | Freedom Vans", "slug": "ep-5-tom-and-kyleigh-how-youtube-has-grown-their-business-freedom-vans-92aa1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-5-tom-and-kyleigh-how-youtube-has-grown-their-business-freedom-vans-92aa1", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, I'm joined by Tom and Kyleigh and their two dogs from Freedom Vans. We discuss their origins, the realities of van life and how YouTube has doubled the waitlist on their production timelines.", "podcast_length": "20:09"}, {"id": "dfc34d40-f931-4e85-a2c1-b13cfbfb73c8", "episode_id": "5496349b-70a7-4e0f-9581-7ca8e78b24ee", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4826", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:02): Hello and welcome to Brand Story. My name is Cole Hilborn, and today I'm sitting down with Steven Matthews, the brand manager at Rocky Mountain Bicycles. We're here in beautiful North Vancouver where it's currently raining. How are you doing, Steven?\n\nStephen Matthews (00:16): Yeah, doing great. Thanks Cole.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:18): Yeah, thanks for being here.\n\nStephen Matthews (00:20): It's always raining in North Vancouver.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:23): I guess that's kind of what you guys are known for, right?\n\nStephen Matthews (00:25): Yeah,\n\nCole Heilborn (00:26): It's part of the\n\nStephen Matthews (00:26): Charm.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:27): Cool. Well, for those of the people who might not know, tell us really quickly what is Rocky Mountain? Who are you guys?\n\nStephen Matthews (00:34): Yeah, Rocky Mountain is a bicycle company from Vancouver. Started in 1981. We've always been in the Vancouver area, and right now we're sitting in our r and d facility, which is in North Vancouver, at the base of the mountains here where we get to ride our bikes, play work on our products, and really enjoy the trails.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:57): So you guys have been here for a while in this location, and one of the things you were mentioning is that the North Vancouver North Shore location has really shaped the brand and who you guys are. How has that played out?\n\nStephen Matthews (01:12): So we have two offices. One is in Quebec, which is our head office, where a lot of the executive team, HR finance is working out of. But then the North Vancouver office is the r and d center, which is where our product team, marketing team and r and d team, so engineers work out of, we build prototypes here that are, we're all mountain bikers in this office. And so we go from concept to building frames actually in this, to testing them on the trails and then refining, reiterating. So being a part of North Vancouver is a great trail system here, and we design our bikes to be able to perform on our local network.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:53): You guys have a pretty impressive history as well. Can you kind of give us the rundown, the highlights of that?", "token_estimate": 541, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Stephen Matthews"], "start_timestamp": "00:02", "end_timestamp": "01:53", "podcast_index": 6, "episode_name": "Ep 6: Stephen Matthews | Race Results Aren't the Only Thing that Matters| Rocky Mountain Bicycles", "slug": "ep-6-stephen-matthews-race-results-arent-the-only-thing-that-matters-rocky-mountain-bicycles", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-6-stephen-matthews-race-results-arent-the-only-thing-that-matters-rocky-mountain-bicycles", "guest_name": "Stephen Matthews", "guest_title": "Brand Manager, Rocky Mountain Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, we chat about the sport of mountain biking, the birth of freeride, and why race results aren't the only thing that matters to a brand like Rocky Mountain.", "podcast_length": "19:08"}]
